\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Wall St edges lower on mixed data; yuan weaker\b0\par\par\uc2 802 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Februar 2014\par 22:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Yuan drops most since October 2011 after \uc2 PBOC\uc2  moves\par \par \uc2 * Wall St edges lower, with record high elusive\par \par \uc2 * Mixed U.S data boosts gold prices\par \par \uc2 By Chuck Mikolajczak\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Feb 25 (Reuters) - Stocks on major world markets were flat on Tuesday, holding near six-year highs as investors discounted mixed U.S. economic data, while the U.S. dollar rose against the yuan on suspicion \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street's benchmark S&P 500 edged lower as it struggled to break a record closing high for a second straight day. MSCI's all world index, which tracks stocks in 45 countries, inched up for the 13th session in 15 to close at its highest level of the year after earlier touching its highest intraday level since December 2007.\par \par \uc2 Recent data has raised questions about the strength of the U.S. economy, though equities investors seem inclined to overlook recent soft data due to severe winter weather across the nation. A closely watched housing survey showed home prices rose slightly more than expected in December, though February consumer confidence fell short of expectations.\par \par \uc2 "We're not being aggressive, since valuations aren't spectacular here, but this certainly isn't a time to run to cash," said \uc2 Rex Macey\uc2 , chief investment officer at \uc2 Wilmington Trust\uc2  in Atlanta, Georgia. "We expect we'll take two steps forward and one step back for a while."\par \par \uc2 Uncertainty over China has stoked worries about a faster-than-projected slowdown in its massive economy, dovetailing with political worries in other big emerging markets like Ukraine, Thailand, Nigeria and Turkey.\par \par \uc2 A hefty $38 billion has been pulled out of emerging markets over the last 17 weeks and $44.2 billion has flowed into developed market equity funds since the start of the year.\par \par \uc2 The yuan continued several weeks of weakness, guided by a series of moves by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at instilling caution in those who for years have been betting on its rise versus other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Tuesday brought a significant acceleration in the move. The yuan's sharpest drop since October 2011 extended its fall in the past week to slightly over 1 percent, amid talk the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been discreetly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot market.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  allows the yuan to move 1 percent above or below a midpoint set daily, but experts believe the recent depreciation is intended to set the stage for a widening of that band to 2 percent or more this year to make it more free moving.\par \par \uc2 In Europe, the pan-regional FTSEurofirst 300 was off 0.02 percent as weakness in mining shares undercut a late attempt to pull the index into positive territory.\par \par \uc2 The euro and benchmark \uc2 German government\uc2  bonds kept to tight recent ranges, with little impact from new \uc2 European Commission\uc2  forecasts that slightly increased its growth estimate for the euro zone to 1.2 percent in 2014, with a further 1.8 percent expansion next year.\par \par \uc2 Inflation was seen at 1 percent this year and 1.3 percent in 2015, still well short of the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target of just below 2 percent. The ECB meets early next month and will be armed with its own in-house forecasts.\par \par \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  pushed back its prediction of a rate cut until April, although it did not rule out a move by the ECB to keep money market liquidity topped up by ending its weekly 'sterilization' of past government bond purchases.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones industrial average fell 27.48 points, or 0.17 percent, to 16,179.66, the S&P 500 lost 2.49 points, or 0.13 percent, to 1,845.12 and the Nasdaq Composite dropped 5.38 points, or 0.13 percent, to 4,287.587.\par \par \uc2 Home Depot Inc\uc2 , up 4 percent to $80.98, was a bright spot, as the largest home improvement chain reported quarterly earnings and gave an upbeat outlook for the spring season. The S&P retail index jumped 1.9 percent.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasuries, the benchmark for global borrowing costs, rose 13/32 in price on the benchmark 10-year note to yield 2.701 percent.\par \par \uc2 Gold prices have pushed to their highest level in four months. Spot gold hit its highest since Oct. 30 at $1,343.40 an ounce and U.S. COMEX gold futures for April delivery settled up $4.70 at $1,342.70 an ounce.\par \par \uc2 Brent crude fell $1.13 to settle at $109.51 a barrel and U.S. oil settled down 99 cents at $101.83, weighed down by forecasts of rising U.S. crude inventories as icy weather eases. \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-2-25T22:42:55.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140225ea2p00gwy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
