\par\par \b \uc2 China Yuan Hits Six-Month Low on \uc2 PBOC\uc2  Guidance -2-\b0\par\par\uc2 495 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Februar 2014\par 11:53\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                                   Vs Parity  Previous \par USD/CNY Central Parity   6.1184               6.1189 \par USD/CNY OTC 0830 GMT     6.1266     0.13%     6.0984 \par             High         6.1310    +0.21% \par             Low          6.1003    -0.30% \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SHANGHAI--China's yuan fell to a six-month low against the U.S. dollar late Tuesday as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's continued efforts to push the Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weaker finally led to a short squeeze in the onshore market, signaling a shift in its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 On the over-the-counter market, the dollar hit an intraday high of 6.1310 late Tuesday, the highest level since Aug. 19, 2013. It was also much higher than Monday's close of CNY6.0984. It traded between CNY6.1003 and CNY6.1310.\par \par \uc2 The Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has depreciated against the U.S. dollar by 1.5% from a record high hit in early January and has fallen by 1.2% so far this year after gaining 2.9% in 2013.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  "is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market and will continue to guide the yuan weaker until onshore investors change their views about the yuan," a trader with a Shanghai-based local bank said.\par \par \uc2 Investors have built up a large amount of positions since the start of this year to profit from the yuan's steady rise and dollar-yuan interest rate differential. They generally expected the yuan to break the 6.0000 psychological level later this year.\par \par \uc2 "We believe that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be accepting of some more weakness in the coming days, given the need to support exporters and external demand as domestic demand weakens," said Dariusz Kowalczyk, an economist at Cr\u233\'00\'E9dit Agricole Credit & Investment Bank.\par \par \uc2 The slide in the yuan pushed the yuan's trading level to converge with the reference exchange rates set by the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  and increased two-way volatility, paving the way for the authorities to widen the yuan's daily trading band, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  set the dollar-yuan reference exchange rate, officially known as the central parity, at 6.1184 Tuesday versus 6.1189 on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  allows the yuan to move 1% above or below the reference exchange rate. The band was last widened in April 2012, when the permitted deviation from the reference rate was 0.5%.\par \par \uc2 Analysts expect Beijing to widen the trading band further and allow a 1.5% or 2% deviation in the next few months.\par \par \uc2 Offshore, one-year dollar/yuan nondeliverable forward contracts rose to 6.1537/6.1580 from 6.1357/6.1394 late Monday, implying a 0.5% fall by the yuan over the next year.\par \par \uc2 In the offshore yuan market in Hong Kong, where the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  floats freely, the dollar was at CNY6.1243 late Tuesday, higher than CNY6.0973 late Monday.\par \par \uc2 Write to Wynne Wang at wynne.wang@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 25-02-14 1053GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020140225ea2p00096\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
