\par\par \b \uc2 Easy  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Bet Gets Harder As The Chinese Yuan Tumbles -3-\b0\par\par\uc2 1,147 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Februar 2014\par 03:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (FROM THE WALL STREET JOURNAL 2/25/14)\par \par \uc2 By Nicole Hong and Fiona Law\par \par \uc2 A sharp and sudden slide in China's yuan is forcing investors to rethink one of the most reliable trades in financial markets over the past four years: betting on gains in the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Since China resumed efforts to move the yuan higher in 2010, traders have piled into bets that profited as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has staged a seemingly relentless advance. But after hitting a record against the dollar in mid-January, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has dropped 1%, reaching a near-four-month low Monday.\par \par \uc2 The size of the recent declines has prompted some to speculate China may be tapping on the brakes, at least for now.\par \par \uc2 "Last week's move has been enough to change attitudes about whether or not we have a one-way [bullish] bet here in the yuan," said Edmund Harriss, a London-based portfolio manager at Guinness Atkinson Asset Management, which oversees $700 million. "\uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is reminding us all that the stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not a given in the short term."\par \par \uc2 Even so, Mr. Harriss boosted his yuan bets after the selloff, predicting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would eventually resume its climb.\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  determines a daily reference point for the yuan, also known as renminbi, then lets it trade 1% higher or lower. Since 2005, it has gradually moved the rate up, allowing the yuan to strengthen 33%. A linked \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , called the "offshore" yuan, trades freely in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 It isn't clear how much the recent decline in the yuan reflects economic fundamentals and how much may reflect a concentrated effort from the Chinese government. The economy slowed somewhat in the fourth quarter, to 7.7% year over year, from 7.8% in the third quarter, which is the way the government measures gross-domestic-product growth. On a quarterly basis the slowdown was even sharper, and analysts say the economy is likely to continue to decelerate in the early part of this year.\par \par \uc2 A number of recent indicators have showed weakness, particularly purchasing-managers indexes, which have indicated broad retrenchment in manufacturing. Trade figures have been bullish, but those numbers may be distorted by exporters overbilling as a way to get more money past China's capital controls.\par \par \uc2 But the yuan "is basically still driven by policy makers rather than market forces," noted \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland Group PLC\uc2 's Louis Kuijs in an analyst note. He said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is "keen to move to more exchange-rate flexibility and volatility."\par \par \uc2 Analysts say China may be trying to shake out short-term speculators, part of a broader effort to reduce the amount of cash sloshing around the country's economy. The move could also be a signal to investors that they should prepare for more volatility in the yuan's exchange rate as the country opens up its financial markets to the world. Wang Tao, an economist for UBS AG, even suggested in a note that "the era of steady [yuan] appreciation may be drawing to a close."\par \par \uc2 While some money managers have held on to their bullish yuan bets, or even added to them, the yuan's recent moves show that, as \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  gradually relaxes its grip on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , one-way wagers on its appreciation are no longer a sure thing.\par \par \uc2 China has halted the yuan's rise before. It kept the exchange rate steady for two years after the financial crisis. And in 2012, the yuan was allowed to sink about 1.5% over a roughly three-month period. Since then, it has risen more or less steadily.\par \par \uc2 Washington has long asked Beijing to give the market a bigger role in the exchange rate, and most of the U.S. political pressure has been for China to let the yuan strengthen, which tends to make American-made goods more competitive by rendering them cheaper by comparison. Policy makers in Washington and other leading economies would be unlikely to criticize \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to push down the yuan unless it led to a substantial depreciation, said David Loevinger, a former Treasury official who is now an analyst at investment firm \uc2 TCW Group Inc\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A spokeswoman for the Treasury Department declined to comment on the recent weakness in the yuan and pointed toward Treasury Secretary Jacob Lew's recent comments. "They're moving towards market-determined exchange rates," Mr. Lew said Friday in Sydney. "As we've noted in our exchange reports over and over, we still raise concerns about it."\par \par \uc2 Officials from Beijing and other members of the \uc2 Group of 20\uc2  biggest economies affirmed Sunday in statement released in Sydney that "exchange-rate flexibility can also facilitate the adjustment of our economies."\par \par \uc2 Investors earned small but predictable returns as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  allowed the yuan to strengthen steadily. Funds that primarily own the yuan and bonds that are priced in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  have returned about 5% over the past year, even as the vast majority of emerging-market funds have lost money, according to \uc2 Morningstar Inc\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The yuan's steady appreciation has also drawn the attention of so-called hot money -- short-term speculators looking to turn a quick profit as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rises.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say it was those traders who were stung by last week's selloff. Many wagered on the yuan via options contracts and other derivatives. These investments magnify profits from small gains in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  but can also lead to big losses when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reverses.\par \par \uc2 "There were way too many structured products bought by private-bank investors and wealthy individuals," said Eric Yum, head of offshore yuan and Hong Kong dollar trading at \uc2 J.P. Morgan Chase & Co\uc2 . "[A] panic selloff was triggered when [China] kept fixing the yuan at weaker levels."\par \par \uc2 Some money managers looking for longer-term investments are also wary of how popular the yuan trade has become. Binqi Liu, a New York-based portfolio manager at \uc2 HSBC\uc2  Global Asset Management, which oversees $420 billion, said she reduced her bets in favor of the yuan in January, as she felt bullish wagers were drawing too much money.\par \par \uc2 "Everybody on the Street likes the renminbi," said Ms. Liu. "Whenever it's this crowded, any change in the market tends to be magnified, and the volatility will be higher. We prefer to stay neutral."\par \par \uc2 Others are viewing the dip as a buying opportunity. They see little sign of a permanent shift in China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, which calls for a gradual loosening of control over the yuan. That suggests a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the future, since many economists believe the yuan's value is set at an artificially low level.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 William Mauldin and Bob Davis contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 25-02-14 0218GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020140225ea2p0002d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
