\par\par \b \uc2 Yuan Weakens: Not A Sell-Off, Seen As PBoC  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  -- Barron's Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 By Shuli Ren   \par \uc2 431 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Februar 2014\par 13:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Chinese yuan fell 0.5% to a six-month low today, the most since November 2010, to close at 6.13 per dollar in Shanghai. Year-to-date, the yuan has fallen 1% against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 While this scale of depreciation is no comparison to what the Turkish lira or the South African rand has gone through in the last nine months, it raised alarms in the marketplace nonetheless. This is because the Chinese yuan has been seen as a one-way bet - that is, up. The yuan appreciated steadily against the dollar by 3% in 2013, and according to Bloomberg, has strengthened in all but three quarters since a dollar peg ended in July 2005 and has advanced 35% since then, making it the best performing emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So how should we interpret this? Don't panic; it is not a sell-off, analysts say. Rather, the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  (PBoC) has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to dispel the market perception of a one-way bet - or at least this is how analysts see it. Wall Street Journal reports:\par \par \uc2 There is also a growing perception that the \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been proactively undermining expectations that the yuan would relentlessly appreciate, traders and analysts say.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  could be engineering the yuan's recent depreciation in an effort to deter speculative capital inflows from investors seeking to profit from China's higher interest rates and expected gains for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 What PBoC wants to engineer is an increased two-way volatility, so unwanted "hot money" does not flow into China to inflate asset prices.\par \par \uc2 And don't forget that China is likely to be betting on a global recovery as well, and as an exporter, its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now a lot more expensive than its competitors, putting it at a disadvantage in international trade. \uc2 UBS\uc2  estimates China's trade-weighted exchange rate against its major trading partners' \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  has appreciated by more than 10% in 2013. For instance, the Taiwan dollar depreciated 2.6% against the U.S. dollar last year. Even though China recorded a trade surplus last year, the more expensive yuan is likely to bite this year, notes \uc2 UBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Year-to-date, the iShares China Large Cap ETF (FXI) lost 8%, the \uc2 iShares MSCI China ETF\uc2  (MCHI) lost 7%. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  More at Barron's Emerging Markets blog, \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.barrons.com/emergingmarketsdaily/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.barrons.com/emergingmarketsdaily/}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 February 25, 2014 07:32 ET (12:32 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140225ea2p001up\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
