\par\par \b \uc2 Sharp Fall in HK Fiscal Surplus Trims Room for Stimulus Measures: HSB -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 2,006 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Februar 2014\par 07:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0629 GMT [Dow Jones] The Hong Kong government will announce a budget surplus of "slightly over HK$10 billion" for the current financial year ending March 31, sharply lower than the surplus of HK$65 billion a year earlier, say people with direct knowledge of the data. Rising social welfare spending and lower income from property transaction charges--following efforts to curb the property boom--are weighing on the government's fiscal strength. "With a dramatic decline in the budgetary surplus, we believe it is too much to expect the government to offer one-off handouts and tax concessions to match the scale and variety of those for last year," says Hang Sang Bank economist Ryan Lam. Last year, the Hong Kong government offered HK$33 billion in one-off stimulus measures. "Resources freed up will be redirected into higher-priority areas, including healthcare and social welfare spending in the long term. We see the combination of these changes to provide a mild boost to domestic consumption, given that social welfare has assumed a more durable form and is hence more likely to have discernible impacts on the economy," Lam adds. Financial Secretary John Tsang is scheduled to announce his budget speech on Wednesday at 0300 GMT. (chester.yung@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0614 GMT [Dow Jones] Gold slips a touch in modest profit taking, with some analysts saying physical demand in China is slowing. Premiums at the \uc2 Shanghai Gold Exchange\uc2  over global prices on Friday fell to $0.75/oz--a level not seen for some time, says Walter de Wet, analyst at Standard Bank. \uc2 OCBC Bank\uc2  says the recent rally in gold prices is due to weaker-than-expected economic data from the U.S. and due to ongoing political turmoil in Ukraine that has helped trigger gold's safe-haven demand. "Still, we opine that these factors are very likely to be short-term stimulus to gold prices and remain bearish on gold prices, given our expectations for the U.S. [Federal Reserve's] bond purchase program to end by the end of 2014," the bank says. Spot gold is at $1,333.80/oz, down $2.80 from its previous close. (arpan.mukherjee@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0603 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares finish 0.2% higher at 8575.62 as a slew of product launches at the Mobile World Congress help boost electronics stocks, a local trader says. Banks and insurers continue their descent on concerns that a proposed hike in the business tax will squeeze their margins and war chest for overseas expansion, he adds. After showcasing new products at the Barcelona mobile conference, low-cost chip designer \uc2 MediaTek\uc2  (2454.TW)--the most actively traded--ends up 2.2% at NT$445.50, while HTC (2498.TW) gains 1.9% to NT$132.00; chip maker TSMC (2330.TW) adds 0.9% to NT$108.00. Cathay Financial (2882.TW), Taiwan's biggest bank, closes down 0.6% at NT$44.45, and Fubon (2881.TW) falls 0.2% to NT$42.20. The trader tips the Taiex to remain in the 8500-8600 band with an upward bias. (aries.poon@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0513 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Hong Kong's economy is likely to grow 3.5% in 2014, according to the median forecast of five economists polled by The Wall Street Journal. The GDP growth forecast this year is better than the expected 3.0% last year, as the improving global economy could boost the city's export growth, economists say. "Given its sizeable trade exposure to advanced economies and sound economic fundamentals, Hong Kong is better positioned to benefit from the global recovery [in 2014], says Hang Seng Bank economist Ryan Lam. Hong Kong Financial Secretary John Tsang is set to announce 2013 GDP growth and the forecast for 2014 GDP growth Wednesday along with his budget speech, which starts at 0300 GMT. (chester.yung@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0509 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  sells Y399.2 billion of 1.9% 40-year bonds in a Dutch style auction. The highest accepted yield was 1.725%, which was a record low and undershot market expectations of 1.735\u65285\'FF\'05, dealers say. While the bid-to-cover ratio worsens to 3.68 from 4.13 in the previous tender in November last year, the result suggests demand is "solid," says Shuichi Ohsaki, a bond strategist at \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2 . But what's behind such demand is hard to tell, he says, because some investors are likely loading up on 40-year bonds just to sell them at better prices to the BOJ, which is widely expected to buy long-term debt through market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday. Lead JGB futures are up 0.07 at 145.17. The yield on 20-year JGBs is down one basis point at 1.450%, while the 30-year rate stands down one and a half basis point at 1.625%. (takashi.nakamichi@wsj.com )\par \par \uc2 0456 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds are higher, boosted by better-than-expected results of a new auction of NT$40 billion of 10-year bonds, two local traders say. The cut-off yield of the 10-year bond is at 1.5830%, near the lower end of market expectations of 1.5800%-1.6000%. "The outcome reflects rising interest from a few local banks, which is helping to boost investor sentiment," a trader says. But he adds those banks may just want to cover their short positions and the bond market is still likely to stay in consolidation mode in the near term. The most-traded 10-year bond yield is at 1.5830%, off an intraday low of 1.5800% hit after the auction vs 1.5935% late Monday. Traders tip near-term support at 1.5750%. The five-year bond yield is at 1.0770% vs 1.0780% late Monday. (fanny.liu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0452 GMT [Dow Jones] More gains could lie ahead for the Singapore dollar if the technical support for USD/SGD on the daily chart at 1.2612 is breached. A Tuesday close below this level would make it more likely that the U.S. unit falls further toward 1.2561 where the next chart support lies. The Singapore dollar, considered a safe haven Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has clawed back losses from late last week when the steep fall of the Chinese yuan dragged majority of regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower. While the yuan continues to fall Tuesday, investors are no longer as bearish on proxy Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Traders are now less worried that the yuan's fall is a sign of risk aversion, and are instead convinced it's an isolated case due to suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yuan. That said, the value of the yuan does have an effect on the Singapore dollar due to the trade-weighted \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  index which the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  uses to determine a fair relative value for its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Thus if the yuan continues to depreciate, the Singapore dollar could struggle to rally. USD/SGD is now 1.2626 from its Monday close of 1.2621. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0443 GMT [Dow Jones] The Indonesian rupiah may rally further in the near term, the USD/IDR Ichimoku Cloud chart suggests. USD/IDR could soon test the 11,500 support now that it has broken the daily Ichimoku Cloud support. A break of 11,500 could bring into sight the round-figure trading barrier at 11,000. Capital inflows into Indonesia continue to bump the rupiah higher despite slight risk aversion in some Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Recent improvement in Indonesia's economic data and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to keep rates unchanged bode well for the country and therefore the rupiah's prospects. Indonesia's 4Q 2013 current-account deficit dropped to nearly 2.0% of gross domestic product versus 3.8% in the third quarter as the government cut back on expensive fuel imports by reducing fuel subsidies to the public. USD/IDR is now 11,630 from its Monday close of 11,640. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0411 GMT [Dow Jones] The baht continues to be immune to escalating violence in Bangkok, rallying even as protesters still try to oust caretaker Prime Minister Yingluck Shinawatra. The daily chart suggests USD/THB could slip lower -- meaning the baht could strengthen versus the U.S. unit -- as the daily Bollinger downtrend channel comes back into effect, capping the dollar at 32.54. The baht rally is likely to accelerate on a daily USD/THB close below 32.27, which is the base of the Ichimoku Cloud support zone. A break of the Cloud support could see USD/THB drop to 32.00 in the near term. The Thai army has been reiterating that it will not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the ongoing protests, despite more deaths over the weekend, thus giving investors some confidence that there is little likelihood of another destabilizing military coup. USD/THB is now 32.47 from its Monday close of 32.51. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0340 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZD/USD is slightly lower after Tuesday's China \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fix, "which was weaker for the yuan than expected and everything else kind of moved in sympathy," says Wespac Bank Senior Strategist Imre Speizer. The NZD/USD is at 0.8327 versus 0.8332 early in New Zealand. Speizer says the pair has support at 0.8310 and resistance at 0.8350. With little data due overnight, any moves will depend on equity markets, he says. "In the absence of data you get classic risk on and risk off moves in equity markets for no rhyme or reason and it will follow whatever equity markets do," he says. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 0330 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  "is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market and it will continue to guide the yuan weaker until onshore investors change their views about the yuan," a Shanghai-based local bank trader says. A fall in the yuan has led to a short squeeze onshore because most traders expected the yuan to break the 6.0000 level later this year, they say. China's yuan hit a six-month low Tuesday morning as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  pushed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to its biggest daily drop in more than three years. The dollar-yuan pair is at 6.1117 vs 6.0984 Monday close after hitting 6.1250--the highest for the pair since Aug. 22. Central parity is set at 6.1184 vs 6.1189 Monday reducing the gap with the onshore trading level. A convergence means more volatility in the yuan and a possible trading band widening, traders say. Offshore, one-year USD/CNY NDFs are up at 6.1402/6.1436 from 6.1357/6.1394 late Monday. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 25 Feb 2014 01:29 EDT  Sharp Fall in HK Fiscal Surplus Trims Room for -2-\par \par \uc2 0329 GMT [Dow Jones] The highest accepted yield for 40-year JGBs at the 0345 GMT auction result will likely to be around 1.735%, traders say. The latest issue reopens the previous No. 6 issue with 1.9% coupon, which was first sold in May last year, and has been reopened in August and November since. Traders say that they expect a smooth auction. "There are many supportive factors, including the feeling of the bonds being in short supply due to the BOJ's massive buying, and extensions of bond indexes," says Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities strategist Katsutoshi Inadome. "The auction should go off without being particularly eventful." Lead March JGB futures are up 0.06 at 145.16 as of the midday close.(eleanor.warnock@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 25, 2014 01:29 ET (06:29 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140225ea2p000sy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
