\par\par \b \uc2 NZD/USD Tad Lower As Yuan Weighs - \uc2 Westpac\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,711 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Februar 2014\par 04:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0340 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZD/USD is slightly lower after Tuesday's China \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fix, "which was weaker for the yuan than expected and everything else kind of moved in sympathy," says Wespac Bank Senior Strategist Imre Speizer. The NZD/USD is at 0.8327 versus 0.8332 early in New Zealand. Speizer says the pair has support at 0.8310 and resistance at 0.8350. With little data due overnight, any moves will depend on equity markets, he says. "In the absence of data you get classic risk on and risk off moves in equity markets for no rhyme or reason and it will follow whatever equity markets do," he says. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @FarroHoward)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0330 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  "is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market and it will continue to guide the yuan weaker until onshore investors change their views about the yuan," a Shanghai-based local bank trader says. A fall in the yuan has led to a short squeeze onshore because most traders expected the yuan to break the 6.0000 level later this year, they say. China's yuan hit a six-month low Tuesday morning as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  pushed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to its biggest daily drop in more than three years. The dollar-yuan pair is at 6.1117 vs 6.0984 Monday close after hitting 6.1250--the highest for the pair since Aug. 22. Central parity is set at 6.1184 vs 6.1189 Monday reducing the gap with the onshore trading level. A convergence means more volatility in the yuan and a possible trading band widening, traders say. Offshore, one-year USD/CNY NDFs are up at 6.1402/6.1436 from 6.1357/6.1394 late Monday. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0329 GMT [Dow Jones] The highest accepted yield for 40-year JGBs at the 0345 GMT auction result will likely to be around 1.735\u65285\'FF\'05, traders say. The latest issue reopens the previous No. 6 issue with 1.9% coupon, which was first sold in May last year, and has been reopened in August and November since. Traders say that they expect a smooth auction. "There are many supportive factors, including the feeling of the bonds being in short supply due to the BOJ's massive buying, and extensions of bond indexes," says Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities strategist Katsutoshi Inadome. "The auction should go off without being particularly eventful." Lead March JGB futures are up 0.06 at 145.16 as of the midday close.(eleanor.warnock@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0321 GMT [Dow Jones] The Australian dollar fell as low as 0.9016 against the greenback in Asian trade Tuesday on contagion from weakness in the Chinese yuan -- the spot USD/CNY touched a six-month high of 6.1250 this morning. Further downside in the Aussie/U.S. dollar pair may encounter support at 0.9005 and 0.8978. Spot AUD/USD was recently at 0.9018 compared to 0.9035 in late New York trade Monday. (jerry.tan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0309 GMT [Dow Jones] The yuan tumbles for a fifth consecutive day as the \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  apparently engineers the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weaker again. Spot USD/CNY gapped higher at its open of 6.1060 versus its Monday close of 6.0984 -- implying a weaker yuan -- and then leapt to a 6-month high of 6.1250 due to stop-loss orders above the round-figure barrier at 6.1000 getting triggered. Clients often leave buy or sell orders near the round-figure due to intrinsic belief that these barriers are more likely to hold. The yuan has been depreciating due to a combination of the PBOC moving the benchmark yuan exchange rate weaker, as well as the liquidation of speculative bets on the yuan as losses mount. Spot USD/CNY is now 6.1131 from its Monday close of 6.0984 - implying a 0.2% loss for the yuan versus the greenback. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0254 GMT [Dow Jones] The Malaysian ringgit surrenders early gains as USD/MYR bounces off technical support at the bottom of the daily Bollinger down-trend channel at 3.2730. Close by, the daily Ichimoku Cloud support at 3.2610 adds to support the U.S. dollar. The ringgit could further weaken toward 3.2980 where the top of the daily USD/MYR Bollinger down-trend channel awaits. The USD/MYR is rebounding as the market -- through the process of price discovery -- finds that USD/MYR is unable to trade any lower due to a lack of fresh sellers. An external factor that may be adding to the ringgit's retreat is the further weakening of the Chinese yuan which has dropped to its lowest level since August 2013. USD/MYR is now 3.2780 from its Monday close of 3.2815. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0208 GMT [Dow Jones] Depreciation of the Chinese yuan -- which is on the cusp of a six-month low against the greenback this week -- is unnerving investors as a sign of things to come, but asset managers expect exporters in Asia where workers are willing to accept lower wages, such as Vietnam, will remain competitive. Marginal \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation will make little difference given high production costs in China's coastal cities, says SooHai Lim, who manages $504.8 million for the Baring ASEAN Frontiers Fund. The potential upside from the Trans-Pacific Partnership is higher for Vietnam than China, not currently party to the agreement, he adds. Policymakers' motivation for allowing the yuan to fall is unclear, but it may be a means of reducing hot money inflows and reforming its \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in the long run, says \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 . "More fundamentally, slow growth is likely to limit near-term appreciation, if any, until the Chinese economy is through its soft patch," the bank adds. (gregor.hunter@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0151 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/TWD is lower, tracking the greenback's weakness against most regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  while exporter sales near the month-end also weigh, says a local trader. The pair is at 30.304, lower than 30.330 before the local \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's likely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday, which lifted it to 30.425 at the close of the onshore trading session, traders say. A trader notes exporters started to sell [the USD] Monday, "but such short-lived month-end selling will not likely change sentiment that still likely favors the greenback," he adds, and tips the pair to trade in a 30.280-30.320 band for the session. The Taiex is up 0.3%. (fanny.liu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0147 GMT [Dow Jones] Gold price remains strong with inflows into physically backed exchange traded funds increasing, Helen Lau, a senior analyst at UOB Kay Hian says in a report. A mixed economic outlook in the U.S. as well as "stalled relationship" between the U.S. and Russia over Ukraine will also raise concerns about rising political risk in the short-term, she says, and adds that these factors will help to underpin prices. Spot gold is in a tight range in early Asia after rising 0.8% overnight. Besides, political risks in Thailand have added to the metal's safe-haven demand. In Thailand, three people were killed and dozens injured Sunday after a grenade was thrown into an area where anti-government protesters had been camping out in the capital, Bangkok. Spot gold is at $1,335.50/oz, down $1.10 from its previous close. (arpan.mukherjee@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0135 GMT [Dow Jones] Tocom rubber futures are rebounding after Monday's selloff as investors buy-on-dips during the low production season. "Still, sentiment in the rubber market is weak ... due concerns about China's growth, so gains may be short-lived," says a Bangkok-based investor. The previous benchmark July rubber trades Y1.8 higher at Y219/kg. The new benchmark August rubber opened at Y219.5/kg and was last at Y219.1/kg. (huileng.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0023 GMT [Dow Jones] Crude-oil futures are mixed in early Asian trade Tuesday after oil markets settled higher overnight. "The first rise in three days for WTI was on speculation of some inventory tightening at Cushing and overnight strength in U.S. equities," ANZ says. "Also of relevance to WTI was a statement from President Obama that he expects a decision on the Canadian leg of the Keystone pipeline in the next couple of months," the bank says. Weekly U.S. oil inventory from the API due later today will provide further cues, with another buildup in overall crude stockpiles expected. Brent is supported by a further drop in Libyan oil production to 230,000 bbl/day over the weekend, and is trading near its highest level this year. Nymex crude is down 19 cents at $102.63/bbl, Brent crude is up 79 cents at $110.64/bbl. (eric.yep@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0018 GMT [Dow Jones] A broad weakness is emerging in base metals markets, \uc2 Barclays\uc2  says after LME 3-month copper finished 1.1% lower at $7,076/ton Monday and aluminum closed 0.5% lower at $1,762/ton. "We expect nickel to extend lower toward multi-month lows near $13,200/ton," \uc2 Barclays\uc2  says. LME 3-month nickel ended down 0.2% at $14,325/ton. Lingering concerns about the future of demand growth in top consumer China could keep a cap on prices. Copper prices were hit hard after the CNY dropped to a four-month low against the USD--a weaker domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could curb factories' ability to buy the raw material, which is traded in USD. Reports that a mid-size Chinese bank temporarily stopped issuing new loans to property developers added to these concerns, analysts say. Copper is widely used in residential and commercial construction, and China's booming real-estate sector has been a key driver of the country's metal demand in recent years. (arpan.mukherjee@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 24, 2014 22:40 ET (03:40 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140225ea2p000hh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
