\par\par \b \uc2 China's  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Reserve Chief Resigns\b0\par\par\uc2 974 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Januar 2014\par 10:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Korean (English)\par \uc2 RTNWK\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 BEIJING--The invisible man behind China's $3.8 trillion foreign cash hoard is disappearing.\par \par \uc2 Zhu Changhong, a former star bond trader in the U.S. who was recruited by Chinese officials about four years ago from investment firm Pimco to manage the country's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, resigned unexpectedly, officials said Tuesday. The move comes as China grapples with boosting returns at a time of increased turbulence in global markets.\par \par \uc2 In a statement to The Wall Street Journal, \uc2 China's State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the division of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that manages the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, said the 44-year-old Mr. Zhu is set to wrap up his mission at the agency at the end of the month.\par \par \uc2 His departure was made "according to plan," the statement said, while adding that Mr. Zhu played "an important and outstanding role" in managing the reserves.\par \par \uc2 SAFE press officials declined to comment beyond the statement. Mr. Zhu, whose title was chief investment officer, declined to comment and referred questions to SAFE.\par \par \uc2 The agency doesn't disclose returns. But Mr. Zhu won praise inside and outside the agency for persuading his superiors to invest more in U.S. corporate bonds, equities and real estate, rather than relying on the safe-but-dull investments in U.S. Treasurys that were SAFE's hallmark. Outside the agency, he was dubbed "the invisible man" by China's media for his unwillingness to appear in public or have his pictures taken.\par \par \uc2 "Zhu's departure is a big loss to SAFE, and to China," says Peng Junming, a former SAFE official who runs his own investment firm, Empire Capital Management.\par \par \uc2 It isn't clear why Mr. Zhu left. But people familiar with his work at SAFE said he had trouble navigating internal politics at the secretive Chinese government agency. His two decades in the U.S. was held against him by some colleagues who felt slighted that he was given such a plum position without having to work his way through the Chinese hierarchy, they said.\par \par \uc2 "Zhu's performance was viewed positively, but there was always some doubt over whether he would fit in longer term," said a Chinese government official.\par \par \uc2 Not all those with long U.S. experience have had similar problems. SAFE's chief, \uc2 Yi Gang\uc2 , taught for years at Indiana University.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Zhu was the right-hand man to investor \uc2 Bill Gross\uc2  at \uc2 Allianz SE\uc2 's \uc2 Pacific Investment Management Co\uc2 ., or Pimco, before Chinese officials recruited him to SAFE in late 2009. SAFE manages the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buys to control the value of the yuan.\par \par \uc2 While at SAFE, Mr. Zhu convinced the agency to bet on Japanese equities in the second half of 2012 before they rose sharply, said individuals familiar with his work, though the size of the position wasn't clear. He also boosted SAFE's investments in European debt when many other investors were wary of its riskiness, the people said.\par \par \uc2 In June 2010, just after Mr. Zhu started at SAFE, about 45% of China's reserves, or $1.11 trillion, were invested in \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  bonds, according to an analysis by ChinaScope Financial, a financial data provider. Since then, China's overall purchases of U.S. debt increased, but Mr. Zhu steadily helped reduce the percentage devoted to Treasurys to about 35%, or $1.29 trillion, in September 2013, the most recent figures available.\par \par \uc2 It isn't clear what Mr. Zhu plans to do next. SAFE officials said the agency's policy forbids ex-employees in certain positions from competing with SAFE for a certain period.\par \par \uc2 His successor will face a quickly changing landscape. The U.S. Federal Reserve is expected to continue tapering bond purchases to stimulate the U.S. economy and perhaps to start to raise interest rates. Economists say those actions should help boost the value of the dollar and make U.S. assets more attractive.\par \par \uc2 Many emerging economies view the Fed's actions with alarm because they fear they would lose the investments needed to pay off their foreign debts. In recent weeks, \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in many developing countries including Argentina, Brazil and Indonesia have fallen, and markets have sunk in part because of fears those economies could get hit.\par \par \uc2 With its vast reserves and largely closed financial system, China is in a different situation. SAFE welcomes the Fed action, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  advisers said, because it might reduce the buildup of China's foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings. For years, China has invested most of its reserves in dollars and seen the value of its holdings sink as the dollar loses its value against the yuan.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Yi, the SAFE chief, has argued publicly that China should take a number of steps to slow the growth of China's foreign reserves or even to reduce them. Those steps include allowing the market a bigger role in determining \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's exchange rate, rather than having the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly to slow its appreciation, and making it easier for Chinese citizens to invest overseas and for foreign capital to come in.\par \par \uc2 If China were to fully open up the so-called capital account, economists at the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  estimate, the overall result would be a net outflow of $1 trillion to $1.6 trillion.\par \par \uc2 But Mr. Yi has yet to convince the leadership to take such steps. Standard Chartered estimates that China's reserves will grow another $400 billion in 2014 largely because it expects the country's trade surpluses to widen.\par \par \uc2 Write to Lingling Wei at lingling.wei@wsj.com and Bob Davis at bob.davis@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 Subscribe to WSJ: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires }}}\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 28, 2014 08:45 ET (13:45 GMT)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 29-01-14 0901GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNWK00020140129ea1t0000n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
