\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2  Yuan up on Strong Fixing but Traders Suspect PBOC  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 508 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Januar 2014\par 10:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  \par                                   Vs Parity  Previous \par USD/CNY Central Parity   6.1008               6.1109 \par USD/CNY OTC 0830 GMT     6.0521    -0.80%     6.0550 \par             High         6.0540    -0.77% \par             Low          6.0500    -0.83% \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SHANGHAI--China's yuan rose against the U.S. dollar Friday after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  guided the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stronger via a daily reference rate--but a record hit by the yuan in the morning was erased, traders said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 A trade of 6.0474 a dollar was seen in systems in the morning around 9:31 a.m. Shanghai time (0131 GMT). That was a record level. However, it was removed before 10 a.m., according to several traders. The lower boundary for the day was also raised to 6.0500--which is the last record for the yuan hit on Jan. 2.\par \par \uc2 On the over-the-counter market the dollar was at CNY6.0521 around 0830 GMT--lower than Friday's close of CNY6.0550. It traded between CNY6.0500 and CNY6.0540, according updated data on trading terminals.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set the daily reference rate at 6.1008 compared with Thursday's 6.1109. Traders speculated it purchased dollars to offset the strong yuan fixing and suspect it also accidentally set the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  too strong.\par \par \uc2 "According to what we heard all the trades under 6.0500 were asked to be cancelled," a Shanghai-based foreign bank trader said adding it is unusual for so many trades to be withdrawn.\par \par \uc2 The China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trade System run by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't replied to a call from The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 China's yuan gained 2.9% against the dollar in 2013. Traders and analysts are expecting the yuan to break the 6.0000 key level later this year. The 6.0000 level can be easily touched with only 500 points away if the PBOC allows the market to trade freely.\par \par \uc2 In the offshore yuan market in Hong Kong, where the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  floats freely, the dollar was at CNY6.0370, lower than CNY6.0383 late Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Offshore, one-year dollar/yuan nondeliverable forward contracts fell to 6.1126/6.1163 from 6.1180/6.1200 late Thursday implying a 1.0% fall by the yuan over the next year.\par \par \uc2 China has tried to hold down the value of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and has accumulated the world's largest foreign reverse. The PBOC probably bought $73 billion dollars of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  in October--the most in three years--and a similar amount in November, according to Capital Economics.\par \par \uc2 Some experts think the pace of China's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  accumulation will increase. Capital Economics says the PBOC could amass another $500 billion over the next year. That is what they think it will take to keep the yuan from rising to more than 5.90 to the dollar compared with 6.10 now.\par \par \uc2 Write to Wynne Wang at wynne.wang@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 10, 2014 04:25 ET (09:25 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140110ea1a000yh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
