\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2  Yuan up on Strong Fixing, but Traders Suspect  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  in Morning\b0\par\par\uc2 1,648 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Januar 2014\par 04:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0332 GMT [Dow Jones] China's yuan rises after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  set the yuan fixing higher, but traders suspect the wrong level was set, as the PBOC \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support the dollar after the market opened. The USD/CNY pair is at 6.0525 versus 6.0550 at Thursday's close. Trading terminals in the morning showed the pair had hit 6.0474, a record high for the yuan, but the daily low for the pair is at 6.0500 for now. Central parity was set at 6.1008 versus 6.1109 Thursday. "I highly suspect that the PBOC wanted to set the central parity at 6.1108 and that they cancelled all trades under 6.0500," says a Shanghai-based foreign bank trader. The China \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Trade System, the trading system under the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , couldn't be reached for comment. Offshore, one-year USD/CNY NDFs are down at 6.1115/6.1180 from 6.1180/6.1200 late Thursday. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0322 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZD/USD is little changed as markets wait "for the U.S. non-farm payrolls data tonight and to take direction from there," says Chris Hunter, corporate dealing manager at \uc2 Western Union\uc2  Business Solutions. "If it (the data) comes in comfortably high, like over 200k, that will assist the U.S. dollar." With the Fed so clear about what it was wanting to do in December, and the minutes reinforcing that, "unless we see a real surprise I don't think we are going to see too much movement," he says. The pair is at 0.8248 vs 0.8244 early this morning. Hunter puts short-term support at 0.8220, with resistance at 0.8270. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0310 GMT [Dow Jones] After apparently hitting a fresh high of 6.0474 to the U.S. dollar the PBOC cancelled all trades below 6.0500--prompting traders to suspect there was an erroneous fixing of the daily benchmark rate. The yuan seemed to have hit a new high of 6.0474--triggering excitement in the market after the PBOC set the benchmark fixing rate at a fresh record of 6.1008. That is sharply higher than Thursday's fixing of 6.1109. Traders are speculating the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  made a mistake in the "big figure" of the fixing--suggesting it should have been 6.1108 instead of 6.1008. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0305 GMT [Dow Jones] The Indonesia rupiah may soon slip to the U.S. dollar again as corporate treasury departments buy the U.S. dollar after Thursday's slight dip, according to an onshore trader. Currently at 12,200 to the dollar, the rupiah is more likely to weaken from here, partly due to technical support for the dollar at 12,180 where the Bollinger uptrend channel bottoms out. Indonesian importers' consistent need to buy the dollar has often been blamed for rupiah weakness. The psyche of the locals is that the rupiah is inherently weak, thus they buy the dollar to pre-hedge future import costs whenever the rupiah strengthens. The mentality that the dollar is going to get more expensive in the near future has been reinforced by a spate of strong U.S. economic data that could bring an earlier end to U.S. quantitative easing--which would lift U.S. bond yields and thereby the dollar. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0252 GMT [Dow Jones] Gold edges higher in cautious trading but sustained gains are unlikely. Gold prices are likely to remain "weak" given positive U.S. jobs data, \uc2 OCBC Bank\uc2  says in a report, referring to a fall in jobless claims. Investors are keenly watching nonfarm payrolls due later Friday, where a potentially strong 200,000 number is likely to depress gold prices further, OCBC says. Gold prices are still on a downtrend in the longer term, analysts say, as economic growth gathers momentum and investors switch to higher-yielding assets. Spot gold is at $1,231.80/oz, up $4.10 from its previous close. (arpan.mukherjee@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0222 GMT [Dow Jones] The ringgit is up against the U.S. dollar Friday, rising to MYR3.2705 from its Thursday closing of MYR3.2750, but the dollar could find strong support near MYR3.2630 as traders position for likely strong U.S. nonfarm payrolls data. The USD/MYR's daily Bollinger downtrend channel at 3.2630 is the first technical support that traders might be eyeing as a good level to buy the dollar. The consensus on the street is that an upbeat jobs report from the U.S. will bolster the case for a further reduction in the U.S. Federal Reserve's bond-buying. If the Fed buys fewer bonds each month, the yield on those bonds will rise and thereby make the dollar more attractive for investors to hold. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0206 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/PHP is moving lower early Friday, weighed down by the Philippines' successful sale of USD-denominated, 10-year bond. Strong demand allowed Philippines to sell $1.5 billion worth of bonds. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair slips to 44.60 after opening Friday at 44.68 and settling late Thursday at 44.68. A local bank trader says the USD/PHP slide may be limited by expectation of strong U.S. nonfarm payrolls, a factor that could quicken Federal Reserve stimulus tapering. Volume is moderately heavy. Traders expect the USD/PHP to stay within the 44.50-45.00 range in the near-term. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0156 GMT [Dow Jones] Ahead of the release of China's trade balance for December, the \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  guides the yuan to its strongest ever level, marking the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's benchmark rate at 6.1008 versus the previous fixing of 6.1109. A trader based in Singapore reckons this could be a hint that the trade data will be exceptionally strong. The logic is that the PBOC may be trying to prime the yuan market for the upbeat data. The yuan hit a new record of 6.0490 to the U.S. dollar in the spot market earlier today, breaking last week's peak of 6.0500.\par \par \uc2 (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0150 GMT [Dow Jones] Crude-oil futures rebound in early Asian trade Friday after settling near a six-month low in overnight floor trade. Financial markets are focused on U.S. nonfarm payroll data due later today as investors are concerned positive data could result in the U.S. Federal Reserve accelerating the withdrawal of economic stimulus that has supported commodity prices. China trade data due later today will also provide direction to oil markets. Brent is supported by reports of continued unrest in North Africa and the Middle East, and supply outages in the North Sea. The market is watching developments in South Sudan where government troops have advanced toward the strategic rebel-held town of Bentiu, a hub for the country's oil industry. Progress in defusing tensions with Iran has failed to have a marked impact on global oil prices partly because of renewed disruptions elsewhere like in Libya, Capital Economics says. Nymex crude is up 69 cents at $92.35/bbl, Brent crude is up 26 cents $106.65/bbl. (eric.yep@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2343 GMT [Dow Jones]--Offshore accounts at six foreign brokerages place net buy orders for 2.6 million Japanese shares overnight, according to traders. The 8th consecutive day of premarket buying may be positive for the market at the open, although the yen value basis of the figures is unknown. Buy orders total 16.9 million shares, with sell orders amounting to 14.3 million shares. (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2333 GMT [Dow Jones]--Nymex crude is likely to consolidate for now after hitting a seven-month low of $91.64/bbl Thursday, Dow Jones technical analysis shows. The daily continuation chart is still negative-biased as the MACD indicator is bearish, the slow stochastic measure is staying suppressed at the oversold level, while the five-day moving average is below the 15-day moving average and declining. A drop below the $91.64 support would target $91.26 (June 3 reaction low), then $90.11 (May 1 reaction low), the psychological $90 line and $87.55 (April 22 low). Resistance is at $92.93 (Thursday's high); a breach would turn the near-term view positive, exposing the upside to the $94.18-$94.22 band (Wednesday's high-Tuesday's high), then to $94.59 (Monday's high), $95.74 (Jan. 3 high), $98.97 (Jan. 2 high, near the 200-day moving average), $99.39 (Dec. 31 high) and $99.87 (100-day moving average). February crude was up 57 cents at $92.23/bbl on Globex. (jerry.tan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 18:18 EST - USD/JPY should trade within 104.30-105.30 during Asia hours with investors likely to remain on the sidelines ahead the release of the US nonfarm payrolls report, says Daisaku Ueno, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Mitsubishi UFJ \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 . "With no major indicators during Tokyo hours, the pair will likely have a hard time finding direction ahead of the jobs data," he says. A strong reading of 250k or more could lead the pair above its Jan. 2 high of 105.45, while a weak reading of around 150k or lower could see it fall back to the Y103 level, he says. DJ/WSJ median forecast is for a 200k gain. USD/JPY last at 104.85. (alexander.martin@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 09, 2014 22:32 ET (03:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140110ea1a000et\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw145\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2175\pichgoal600
