\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2 's yuan flat as a board as  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  hobbles market\b0\par\par\uc2 704 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 November 2013\par 06:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 By Pete Sweeney SHANGHAI, Nov 26 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market was anemic on Tuesday as activity by major Chinese banks erased market volatility, keeping the yuan trading within a miniscule six-point range in the morning session. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set its official midpoint rate slightly weaker Tuesday morning following a rise in the dollar index overnight, but the setting of the guidance rate appears increasingly academic in recent weeks as the traded rate has remain locked in place. The spot yuan moved between 6.0920 and 6.0926 per dollar in the morning, continuing a trend of extraordinarily range-bound trade that has typified the market - with the exception of a record-setting rally in October - since late August. Traders say that on the behest of regulators, major state-owned banks are moving to buy up excess dollars in the market, effectively holding it back from pricing the yuan more aggressively.\par \par \uc2  The yuan has already surpassed earlier market predictions that Beijing would not allow it to strengthen past 6.1 to the dollar this year. Economists are divided as to whether more appreciation is in store. At this month's economic planning meeting, Chinese leaders committed to further liberalisation of the yuan market, and many expect the \uc2 People's Bank of China (PBOC) \uc2 to widen the yuan's trading band - currently restraining the exchange rate from within 1 percent above or below the midpoint - before the end of the year. "A more laissez-faire \uc2 PBOC\uc2  policy towards \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a potential widening of the USD/CNY trading band will likely accelerate inflows and reinforce this year's strong CNY trend," researchers at \uc2 Barclays\uc2  wrote in a note distributed to clients on Tuesday. However, commitments to allowing more volatility and giving market forces a bigger role in setting the rate have been made many times before, and traders say that at present the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  continues to do the opposite, keeping the yuan market on an extremely even keel. Not everyone believes the bullish sentiment is sustainable. Dariusz Kowalcyzk, strategist at \uc2 Credit Agricole\uc2  CIB, says the increase of tension stemming from China declaring a "Air Defense Identification Zone" over territories Japan administers is bearish for the yuan. "The risk of actual incidents is on the rise and will make it difficult for the CNY and the CNH to rise further in the offshore markets in the near term," he wrote in a research note. However, the offshore yuan market continues to price the CNH at a premium to its onshore cousin at 6.0798 per dollar, implying expectations for further appreciation. The onshore spot yuan market at a glance: \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Item                                Current  Previous  Change\par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  midpoint                       6.1357   6.1342    -0.02%\par  Spot yuan                           6.0923   6.0926    0.00%\par  Divergence from midpoint*           -0.71%             \par  Spot change ytd                                        2.27%\par  Spot change since 2005 revaluation                     35.85%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 *Divergence of the dollar/yuan exchange rate. Negative number indicates that spot yuan is trading stronger than the midpoint. \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 allows the exchange rate to rise or fall 1 percent from official midpoint rate it sets each morning. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     OFFSHORE CNH MARKET   