\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar slips as China says to make yuan more flexible\b0\par\par\uc2 720 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 November 2013\par 17:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  plan to exit \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  seen as dollar-negative\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     * Overarching theme is still Fed tapering\par     By Gertrude Chavez-Dreyfuss\par     NEW YORK, Nov 19 (Reuters) - The dollar edged lower on\par Tuesday in choppy trading after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said it\par would gradually exit from regular \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market.\par     But analysts said over the medium term, the dollar is still\par expected to perform better than the euro and yen as the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal\par Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  prepares to wind down its stimulus, a process that many\par expect will get under way in March next year.\par     The market, meanwhile, digested news on Tuesday that didn't\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 include the Fed. \uc2 Zhou Xiaochuan\uc2 , head of the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2 , said in a book on reforms published on Tuesday that China will gradually expand the yuan trading band to help make the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  more flexible and market-driven. This followed reform plans last week to let the market play a "decisive" role in the\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par economy.  \par     The PBoC comments spurred some modest dollar selling, as the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 widening of the Chinese trading band meant the yuan would strengthen against the dollar. But analysts said the remarks don't mean that China will move the trading band overnight. It could happen though over the next five years. "All announced moves from China over the past eight years toward further flexibility have been followed by a marked reduction in the value of the dollar index," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Douglas Borthwick\f28 \uc2 \f2 , managing director at Chapdelaine \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in New York. He added that a convertible yuan is a condition for the yuan to be included in the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2 's special drawing rights basket. "Once convertibility is achieved, reserve managers will flock to the yuan as an additional reserve \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Its position will take market share away from other overweight reserve \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , most notably the U.S. dollar." The dollar index, a gauge of the greenback's value against six major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was down 0.2 percent at 80.682. The Australian dollar rallied on the China news, rising 0.3 percent to US$0.9413. The euro was up 0.2 percent on the day at $1.3529, recovering from earlier lows of $1.3486. Traders said the German ZEW survey weighed slightly on the euro earlier. Although the economic sentiment reading hit its highest level in four years in November, the survey's current conditions index fell and was below forecasts. "The euro has been hamstrung by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's decidedly easy stance on policy to safeguard a fragile economic rebound," said Joe Manimbo, senior market analyst at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Western Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  Business Solutions in Washington. The dollar, however, has been under pressure since remarks last week by Fed chair nominee \f28 \uc2 \f2 Janet Yellen\f28 \uc2 \f2  were widely interpreted as confirming her dovish stance. Since Yellen's remarks at her Senate confirmation hearing, \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yields have fallen, dragging down the dollar index, which is correlated to bond yields. But even though Yellen said the stimulus would be in place for some time and those comments were negative for the dollar, many market participants believe the greenback would start rallying before the Fed actually starts reducing its asset purchases. "We're generally bullish on the dollar in the bigger picture even though we don't expect the Fed to taper until March 2014," said Greg Moore, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 TD Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Toronto. "People would start buying the dollar in anticipation of that tapering." On Monday, Federal Reserve Bank of New York President \f28 \uc2 \f2 William Dudley\f28 \uc2 \f2  said he was "more hopeful" about the U.S. economy but also said he expected "very accommodative" monetary policy to be in place "for a considerable period of time". Investors were looking ahead to Fed chairman \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ben Bernanke\f28 \uc2 \f2 's remarks after the market close on Tuesday and Wednesday's release of the minutes from the Fed's October meeting for clues to how long it will maintain its monthly $85 billion of bond purchases. Any suggestion that the Fed might be a few meetings away from dialing down on stimulus should support the greenback.\f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 5) | C | D | E | M | O | U | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL | AUF | CANT | J | RBN | ABX | BNX | FUN | UKI | Z | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020131119e9bj0014m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
