\par\par \b \uc2 METALS-Copper bounces from lows on fund buying, weaker dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 715 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 November 2013\par 02:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Speculators buy back short positions\par \par \uc2 * Dollar weakens after \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  says to exit \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Lead, zinc global markets tightening\par \par \uc2 By Silvia Antonioli and Eric Onstad\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Nov 19 (Reuters) - Copper bounced on Tuesday from its lowest levels in more than three months as funds bought back short positions, the dollar weakened slightly and China detailed more of its reforms.\par \par \uc2 Copper was unlikely to rise much further, however, because of uncertainty about the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's plans for its stimulus programme, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 "There's been some short covering because the market held above support at $6,900 (a tonne)," said analyst Andrey Kryuchenkov at VTB Capital. "But I don't think this (move higher) will last."\par \par \uc2 Three-month copper on the London Metal Exchange rebounded from a session low of $6,910 a tonne, its weakest since Aug. 7, touching a intra-day peak of $7,005. It closed virtually flat, down 0.1 percent at $6,970.\par \par \uc2 At the lows, copper had shed about 6 percent over the past four weeks after breaking out of its $300 range.\par \par \uc2 Investors sold copper on prospects of less liquidity in the markets if the Fed tapers its commodity-friendly monetary stimulus programme and on expectations of more supply next year.\par \par \uc2 "The small rebound from the new lows this morning was due to opportunistic buying, but uncertainty over stimulus tapering is still dominating headlines and it will still happen in 2014," Kryuchenkov added.\par \par \uc2 Top Fed officials from opposite sides of the policy spectrum pointed to improvement in the U.S. economy on Monday, adding more weight to the notion that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is moving closer to reducing the pace of its $85 billion-a-month asset purchases.\par \par \uc2 Scaling down the stimulus programme would reduce liquidity available to businesses and to commodity investors, eroding price support for metals.\par \par \uc2 Helping support metals was a slightly softer dollar after the \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would gradually withdraw from regular \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 A weaker dollar makes metals priced in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  cheaper for buyers outside the United States.\par \par \uc2 MOUNTAIN OF COPPER\par \par \uc2 Copper is also under pressure from the prospect of rising supply, with a top miner having agreed a 31 percent increase to processing fees for 2014. Miners tend to pay higher processing fees when there is more supply.\par \par \uc2 "We continue to see supply outstripping demand over the next 24 months, which will mean any price rallies for copper will be short-lived," \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  said in a note.\par \par \uc2 In other metals, the global lead market was in deficit by 46,000 tonnes in the first nine months of the year, compared with a surplus of 80,000 a year earlier, the Lisbon-based \uc2 International Lead and Zinc Study Group\uc2  (ILZSG) said.\par \par \uc2 ILZSG data also showed the global zinc market surplus had shrunk by more than half year on year to 38,000 tonnes.\par \par \uc2 "In both cases, this is attributable to significantly increased demand, which clearly outstripped the growth in supply. The situation on both markets is likely to tighten further as a number of larger mines reach their end-of-life over the next few years and insufficient replacements are available," \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  said in a note.\par \par \uc2 Zinc, used to galvanise steel, closed up 0.6 percent to $1,896 a tonne, battery material lead added 0.5 percent to end at $2,092, while tin gained 0.2 percent to $22,800.\par \par \uc2 Nickel finished 0.3 percent higher at $13,630 a tonne.\par \par \uc2 "Nickel tested its early October lows, but has since stabilised and is looking to be forming a floor once again," Leon Westgate at Standard Bank said in a note.\par \par \uc2 Aluminium failed to trade in closing activity and was last bid at $1,793 a tonne, up 0.56 percent.\par \par \uc2 PRICES\par \par \uc2 Three month LME copper\par \par \uc2 Most active ShFE copper\par \par \uc2 Three month LME aluminium\par \par \uc2 Most active ShFE aluminium\par \par \uc2 Three month LME zinc\par \par \uc2 Most active ShFE zinc\par \par \uc2 Three month LME lead\par \par \uc2 Most active ShFE lead\par \par \uc2 Three month LME nickel\par \par \uc2 Three month LME tin\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-METALS/ (UPDATE 6) | ABX | BNX | FUN | RBN | ABN | C | MTL | Z | J | UKI | D | E | M | O | U | GRO | SOF | OIL | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020131119e9bj0002g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
