\par\par \b \uc2 *China to Gradually Widen Yuan Trading Band -- PBOC Chief\b0\par\par\uc2 661 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 November 2013\par 11:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 19 Nov 2013 05:19 EDT *China to Fully Liberalize Interest Rates Over Medium Term -- PBOC Chief\par \par \uc2 19 Nov 2013 05:21 EDT *China to Speed up Capital Account Opening -- PBOC Chief\par \par \uc2 19 Nov 2013 05:26 EDT *China to Relax Cross-Border Institutional Investment Controls -- PBOC Chief\par \par \uc2 19 Nov 2013 05:27 EDT *China to Set up Fund to Back Bank Deposit Insurance Program -- PBOC Chief\par \par \uc2 19 Nov 2013 05:36 EDT *PBOC Chief's Statements From Newly Published Book on China Reforms\par \par \uc2 19 Nov 2013 05:48 EDT *\b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  PBOC to 'Basically' Phase out Routine \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Market -- PBOC Chief\par \par \uc2 19 Nov 2013 05:53 EDT *China to Open Local Bond Market to More Domestic, Foreign Investors -- PBOC Chief\par \par \uc2 19 Nov 2013 05:54 EDT *China Aims to Boost Bond Trading -- PBOC Chief\par \par \uc2 19 Nov 2013 06:58 EDT  China to Gradually Widen Yuan Trading Band -- PBOC Chief\par \par \uc2 BEIJING--China will gradually widen the yuan's daily trading band and "basically" phase out routine \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the head of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a newly published book.\par \par \uc2 Zhou Xiaochuan\uc2 , governor of the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2 , also repeated previous statements that China will speed up the opening of its capital account, which covers investments.\par \par \uc2 The statements were in an article included in a newly published book designed to explain and amplify the decisions taken at the Communist Party's key policy-setting meeting last week. Other senior officials, including Finance Minister Lou Jiwei, also contributed to the book, which boasted a stellar editing team that included President and Communist Party chief Xi Jinping.\par \par \uc2 "We will in an orderly manner expand the renminbi [yuan] trading band according to the development of the [\b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] market and the economic and financial situation," Mr. Zhou wrote.\par \par \uc2 "We need to strengthen the two-way flexibility of the renminbi exchange rate" and keep the rate at a basically stable and reasonable level, he added.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  currently allows the yuan to move 1% above or below a parity rate it sets every day against the dollar.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials have said they want to widen the band for trading against the dollar but have not announced any firm timetable. Mr. Zhou's article did not give one either.\par \par \uc2 Foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders said they did not expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to move immediately on any band widening.\par \par \uc2 "There have been expectations of exchange-rate reforms, including the widening of the yuan band, though any imminent move would be a surprise," said a Beijing-based trader at a local bank.\par \par \uc2 The trader also noted that the previous move to widen the yuan's trading band in April 2012 was preceded by volatile trading when the dollar-yuan consistently hit its daily limit, which was then 0.5%.\par \par \uc2 On the over-the-counter market, the dollar was at CNY6.0927 at Tuesday's close, higher than Monday's close of CNY6.0920. It traded in a narrow range of CNY6.0910 to CNY6.0933.\par \par \uc2 Mr Zhou said China would relax approval requirements for cross-border investment movements under the Qualified Foreign Institutional Investor and Qualified Domestic Institutional Investor programs.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief also said the bank's medium-term goal was to fully liberalize interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Zhou also said China would set up a fund to back a planned bank deposit insurance program.\par \par \uc2 China currently has no bank insurance covering savings, and regulators are concerned that interest-rate liberalization could cut into profits of smaller banks. Deposit insurance is needed to protect depositors in case of a bank failure.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Zhou also said that China wants to boost bond trading and reduce the role of investors who buy bonds and hold them until they mature.\par \par \uc2 Write to Liyan Qi at liyan.qi@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 19, 2013 06:58 ET (11:58 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020131119e9bj001r6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
