\par\par \b \uc2 Chinese Yuan Hits New Highs -- WSJ Blog -2-\b0\par\par\uc2 441 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Oktober 2013\par 09:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (This story has been posted on The Wall Street Journal Online's MoneyBeat blog at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/moneybeat/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/moneybeat/}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 By Ewen Chew\par \par \uc2 China's yuan climbed to a record high and is on track to post its best weekly jump since May, cementing its place as the top-performing Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year, helped by a falling dollar and speculation Beijing may tolerate a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The 0.42% advance this week is notable given all of last month the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moved within a 0.1% range. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  generally keeps a tight rein on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements, allowing the yuan to trade within a 1% range of the daily benchmark rate.\par \par \uc2 The Chinese yuan reached a new high of 6.0953 against the U.S. dollar Thursday as the greenback eased broadly following the passing of the U.S. debt ceiling deal. Though \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set the yuan's daily benchmark rate slightly weaker than the previous day, the yuan rallied on onshore inflows, pushing the value of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  past the previous record of 6.0965 set on Wednesday. The 1% daily trading band that the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  sets for the yuan will cap today's gains at around 6.0817.\par \par \uc2 One trader said the sudden pace of gains may be because the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  is stepping back from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  so heavily in the spot market to stem the yuan's strength.\par \par \uc2 China has long been accused of keeping the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  artificially low to boost exports, but that could be changing as demand for made-in-China products slows as the global economic recovery stutters. Evidence for this was most recently seen as the country's trade surplus slipped in September to $15.2 billion from $28.5 billion the previous month.\par \par \uc2 With China keen to rebalance its economy towards more domestic consumption and less focus on keeping exports competitive, a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may now be more acceptable to the authorities. A stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  increases the purchasing power of consumers, which would help fuel the retail sector and lead to trickle-down effects for the larger economy.\par \par \uc2 So far this year the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is up 2.2% against the greenback for 2013, a mile in front of first runner-up the South Korea won, which has gained 0.5%, and a stark contrast to all other Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  which have fallen this year as growth has slowed in much of the region.\par \par \uc2 For continuously updated news from The Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 17-10-13 0726GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020131017e9ah00087\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw145\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2175\pichgoal600
