\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 China Intervened \highlight\uc2  Aggressively in  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Markets in Latest Quarter\b0\par\par\uc2 By Shen Hong   \par \uc2 615 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Oktober 2013\par 06:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones Korean Newswires English Content\par \uc2 RTNWK\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI--Beijing has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading lately to blunt a rise in the yuan's value, data indicates--a signal, analysts said, of continued caution in liberalizing its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.\par \par \uc2 The data, showing a sharp increase in foreign reserves in the past quarter, follows the opening late last month of a free-trade zone in Shanghai that China vows will be used to accelerate tough changes such as relaxing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and interest-rate policies. These are seen as essential to achieving authorities' goal of a more consumption-driven economy.\par \par \uc2 The fast accumulation of reserves is also evidence that yield-hungry international capital continued to favor China even during a broader selloff in emerging-market assets, analysts said. Investors fled those assets over concerns the U.S. would be scaling back its monetary-easing program, sending U.S. interest rates higher.\par \par \uc2 According to data released Monday, China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves reached $3.66 trillion at the end of the third quarter, up from $3.50 trillion at the end of the second quarter--an "astonishing" pace, research firm Capital Economics said, calling the $160 billion increase "one of the largest increases on record." Reserves rose $47.1 billion in the second quarter and $157.1 billion in the first.\par \par \uc2 Based on the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2 's balance sheet for July and August, Capital Economics estimated the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchased roughly $70 billion from the market in September, compared with $16 billion in both July and August.\par \par \uc2 "The further expansion of foreign reserves suggests there have been capital inflows back into China because of the recent recovery in the country's economic growth as well as faster gains in Chinese property prices," said Steve Wang, research director at Reorient Financial Markets.\par \par \uc2 As emerging economies like Indonesia and Brazil experienced selloffs in stocks and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past few months, the yuan defied the trend to accelerate its pace of appreciation. The dollar was quoted at 6.1026 yuan Tuesday, down from 6.1079 at Monday's close. The yuan, which has gained 2.1% against the dollar this year, touched a record high of 6.1007 to the dollar earlier Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , also known as the renminbi, rose 1% against the U.S. dollar last year.\par \par \uc2 While the capital inflows demonstrated renewed foreign confidence in the Chinese economy, the latest data suggested a wary \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Beijing, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 "In a period when central banks in other emerging markets were \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to shore up their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \uc2 People's Bank\uc2  was acting aggressively to keep the renminbi weak," said Capital Economics.\par \par \uc2 A senior trader at a large Chinese bank in Shanghai said he noticed signs of such frequent and large-scale dollar purchases by the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  in recent weeks. "It's pretty clear that the authorities don't want the yuan to rise too fast," he said.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  purchases suggest an eagerness to strengthen the foundation of the economic recovery in the past quarter, Reorient's Mr. Wang said--especially given signs of renewed weakness in China's exports.\par \par \uc2 Chinese policy makers, Capital Economics said, remain "deeply uncomfortable with allowing market forces a say in determining the exchange rate at times of uncertainty."\par \par \uc2 "The launch of the Shanghai Free Trade Zone last month signaled that policy makers still see opening of capital controls as an important goal, " the London-based research firm added. "But their actions underline that it remains a long way off."\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 15, 2013 04:53 ET (08:53 GMT)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 16-10-13 0403GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNWK00020131016e9ag0000b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
