\par\par \b \uc2 Giant Foreign  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Purchases Show China Still Manipulating Yuan -- WSJ Blog -2-\b0\par\par\uc2 753 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Oktober 2013\par 03:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Newswires English Content\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (This story has been posted on The Wall Street Journal Online's MoneyBeat blog at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/moneybeat/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/moneybeat/}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 By Michael J. Casey\par \par \uc2 China added $166 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves last quarter, money that it most likely placed in \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  debt right as Washington lawmakers began toying with the threat of default.\par \par \uc2 Beyond the awkward timing, the other takeaway from the biggest increase in China's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stockpile since 2011 is that the government of the world's second-largest economy is still heavily manipulating its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan. That leaves it exposed to foreign criticism.\par \par \uc2 A \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  increases its reserves by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to buy dollars, an action that prevents its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from rising. In other words, if it weren't for the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  -- whose reserves have surged $350 billion since the end of last year to $3.66 trillion now -- the yuan would have gained even more than its 3.5% increase versus the dollar this year and even more again against various embattled emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The real question is: Why is this happening?\par \par \uc2 In previous years, upward pressure on the yuan and the accompanying reserve accumulation were driven by current-account flows. Back then, money flooded into China because of its dominance in global exports and because of its high-saving citizens' limited spending on imports. But China's current-account surplus has declined significantly from 10% of GDP in 2008 to a much more manageable 2.5% now, suggesting the country's savings-and-spending imbalance with the rest of the world has eased.\par \par \uc2 That can only mean that the inflow forcing the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  to hoover up dollars is coming through the capital account. It is investors, not exporters, who are bringing in all that money--and they are doing so despite various capital controls designed to block them.\par \par \uc2 But, again, why?\par \par \uc2 Well, one reason could lie in the circular relationship between \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  control of its financial system, its pledges to unwind that over time, and the expectations this generates among investors.\par \par \uc2 China's government has signaled it wants to gradually open its capital account, subject its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to market forces and liberalize the domestic financial system. Given that China had previously kept its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  undervalued to support its exporters and had kept interest rates artificially low to encourage capital expenditure, investors have heard a message that both the yuan and Chinese interest rates will creep higher over time.\par \par \uc2 That's kind of like a sure thing and it's an attractive proposition for investors who've been spooked by volatility in other emerging markets, where fears over a reduction in monetary stimulus from the U.S. Federal Reserve have prompted massive outflows from India, Indonesia, Brazil and Turkey. It's also extremely appealing to residents of Hong Kong, who would otherwise be locked into a Hong Kong dollar that's pegged to the weakening U.S. dollar and to the near-zero interest rates that come with it. Hong Kong residents assume the yuan will eventually be the territory's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so it makes sense to funnel their savings into yuan--whether into offshore accounts now available to them or through loopholes in the capital controls that give them access to higher-paying mainland bank accounts.\par \par \uc2 The Catch-22 for China's leaders is this: As they try to convince U.S. and other foreign government critics that the yuan is now fairly valued, their plan to gradually liberalize the financial system undermines their case. That's because it leaves investors convinced that the yuan has further to run -- that it is still undervalued.\par \par \uc2 "These numbers certainly call into question what the Chinese authorities haves said over the last two or three years" about the yuan's valuation, says \uc2 Nicholas Lardy\uc2 , a China specialist at the Peterson Institute for International Economics.\par \par \uc2 But here's the rub: until the authorities fully liberalize their financial system, there will be no market mechanism with which to gauge that "equilibrium" level. If that means the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  has to keep stockpiling dollars and buying unwanted low-yielding \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  bonds, the push to create one through market reforms could get louder.\par \par \uc2 -- Follow Michael J. Casey on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @mikejcasey.\par \par \uc2 For continuously updated news from The Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 16-10-13 0145GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020131016e9ag0001m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
