\par\par \b \uc2 Yuan trades up, spot price closest to guidance rate in 12 months\b0\par\par\uc2 750 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 2013\par 06:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 By Pete Sweeney SHANGHAI, Oct 8 (Reuters) - The Chinese yuan rose slightly on Tuesday, with the spot price trading only 0.37 percent stronger than the official guidance rate, the narrowest gap in over a year, suggesting regulators' attempts to align the market with the policy rate are gaining traction. Many economists have argued that the bank needs to get the traded rate in line with the midpoint before it can introduce more volatility, and by extension more risk, into the market. The yuan has continued to rise against the dollar, which traders say reflects both a long-term trend and a short-term reaction to the slide of the dollar index in reaction to the ongoing budget impasse in the United States.\par \par \uc2  However, the rate of the rise has slowed since August and intraday volatility has been distinctly muted, which traders say is due to the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  (PBOC) \uc2 using the major state-owned banks to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, restraining the market by their ability to effectively dictate rates through massive transactions conducted amongst each other. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set the official midpoint at 6.1415 per dollar on Tuesday morning when markets reopened after a week-long holiday, up slightly from the last fix of 6.1480 on Sept. 30. The spot yuan changed hands at 6.1189 at mid-morning, mildly stronger than the last close of 6.1220. The onshore spot yuan market at a glance: \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par      \par  Item                      Current    Previo  Change (pct)\par                                       us      \par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  midpoint             6.1415     6.1480  +0.11\par  Spot yuan                 6.1189     6.1220  +0.05\par  Divergence from           -0.37              \par  midpoint* (pct)                              \par  Spot change ytd                              +1.82\par  Spot change since 2005 revaluation           +35.26\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 *Divergence of the dollar/yuan exchange rate. Negative number indicates that spot yuan is trading stronger than the midpoint. \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 allows the exchange rate to rise or fall 1 percent from official midpoint rate it sets each morning. OFFSHORE CNH MARKET A report from international payments communications platform SWIFT showed the yuan was the 8th most traded \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the world as of August, with a 1.49 percent market share. However, as a world payments \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  it lost ground to the Thai baht in August, which displaced it to 12th place, down from 11th place in July. This follows a similar report by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank for International Settlements\f28 \uc2 \f2  published in September, showing that yuan turnover in global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trading surged to make the yuan the 9th most actively traded \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in 2013, driven primarily by growth in offshore yuan trading. The SWIFT report said that 60 percent of yuan trading is done out of London, followed by the U.S. and France, which overtook Singapore as a popular yuan trading centre in June. The recent launch of a new free trade zone in Shanghai is ultimately expected to compete with existing offshore centres, in particular Hong Kong, for yuan deposits and other offshore yuan services, but without damaging the overall growth of the market. In addition, there are questions about how quickly and effectively regulators will be able to implement reforms within\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the zone.                \par         \par The offshore yuan market at a glance:    \par  Instrument                   Current   Difference from onshore\par                                         (pct)\par  Offshore spot yuan           6.1114    +0.12*\par  Offshore non-deliverable     6.1825    -0.66**\par  forwards                               \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 *Premium for offshore spot over onshore **Figure reflects difference from \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's official midpoint, since non-deliverable forwards are settled against the midpoint. . \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     \par     >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>\par     RECENT DEVELOPMENTS\par     - Shanghai FTZ no threat to HK just yet \par     - CHINA MONEY - \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  preparing market for more yuan\par volatility \par     - ANALYSIS-Bullish yuan herd leaves China fundamentals in\par the dust \par     - \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  war or no, Beijing doesn't want Asia to take\par stable yuan for granted   \par         \par     KEY DATA POINTS\par     - Gap between \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  midpoint and spot rate is narrowing.\par GRAPHIC: \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/qyx74t"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/qyx74t}}}\uc2 \f2 \par     - China's trade surpluses mainly driven by weak imports\par rather than strong exports. GRAPHIC: \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/qav68s"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/qav68s}}}\uc2 \f2 \par     - Corporate \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  purchases in July show reduction in yuan\par appreciation expectations. GRAPHIC: \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/tyx74t"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/tyx74t}}}\uc2 \f2 \par     - Hot money outflows reach record high in July GRAPHIC: \f28 {\