\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Emerging-Market  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Gain on Better Asia Data\b0\par\par\uc2 By Prabha Natarajan   \par \uc2 485 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 2013\par 04:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Data that suggested an improved outlook for China's economy triggered a rally in emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday.\par \par \uc2 Some market participants also see it as a continuation of the positive momentum in these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that began Friday after the poor jobs report cast doubt on the strength of the recovery of the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 The most-battered \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Indian rupee and the Brazilian real have recovered more than 1% in the day's trading after China reported that its exports rose in August. The past couple of sessions have helped emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  recover some of their previous losses in August, which saw these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciate more than 20%. However, some analysts warn that the current gains may be temporary and would fade heading into the Federal Reserve's meeting next week. Many market participants anticipate that the Fed, at that time, would announce it is tapering its bond-buying program. Such a move is expected to sop up much of the funds flowing into developing nations.\par \par \uc2 "Emerging markets...are not more vulnerable to Fed tapering than developed economies, and their fundamentals are not seriously deteriorating," said Jan Dehn, head of research at \uc2 Ashmore Investment Management Ltd\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Such a sentiment has prompted investors to buy \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of countries ranging from Brazilian real to the Mexican peso.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real was trading at BRL2.2762, its strongest level in more than four weeks, and up 1.3% on the day to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the improved data from \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a main trading partner with Brazil, has helped the real strengthen its recent recovery.\par \par \uc2 Turkey, another current account deficit country, is clawing back to 2.02-lira levels after surging to all-time highs last week after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  comments that it would not support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The dollar currently fetches TRY2.0258, down 1.2% on the day, according to CQG data.\par \par \uc2 The Indian rupee currently is trading below INR65 to the dollar, a critical level that many analysts believe will provide the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  support from buyers, who from here, would expect the rupee to gain to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Other Asian trading partners of China, such as Malaysia also saw a recovery in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to MYR3.2923 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, some emerging-market countries are using this window of opportunity to raise much-needed dollar-denominated debt at higher prices. South Africa and Russia led the charge on Monday with new bond offerings.\par \par \uc2 South Africa is raising $2 billion in debt at an anticipated yield of 315 basis points over comparable Treasury yields. Russia, meanwhile, is selling nearly $7 billion in debt through various tranches and with a euro-denominated part as well.\par \par \uc2 Write to Prabha Natarajan at prabha.natarajan@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020130910e99a000rt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw