\par\par \b \uc2 DJ OPINION: Beijing's War on Shadow Banking\b0\par\par\uc2 By MARK A. DEWEAVER   \par \uc2 687 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juli 2013\par 01:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 On June 20, \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  precipitated a major credit crisis by withholding funds from the nation's cash-starved banking system. \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2 's refusal to act as liquidity provider of last resort froze lending in the interbank market. Overnight rates, which had been as low as 2.1% in early May, exploded, closing at a record 13.4%.\par \par \uc2 As rumors swirled about the solvency of China's state-owned banks, some commentators began talking about a Chinese "Lehman moment." But the crisis passed and the overnight interbank lending rate quickly came back to earth. By July 4, it had fallen to 3.4%.\par \par \uc2 Trouble in the interbank market had been brewing since early June, when Beijing began a crackdown on illicit inflows of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , previously a major source of growth in the local money supply. The demand for yuan also began rising, as Chinese banks prepared for their June 30 book closings and their customers for their first-half tax payments.\par \par \uc2 The result was a growing imbalance between the supply of and demand for credit. As rumored large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly failed to materialize, commercial banks realized they would have to fend for themselves. Lenders hoarded cash to guard against potential counterparty defaults, and the normal flow of funds among financial institutions quickly dried up.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's immediate objective seems to have been to rein in China's "shadow banking" system, which has grown rapidly in recent years and now accounts for a significant share of total Chinese credit. Shadow banking in China involves lightly regulated products that allow savers to earn more than the official deposit rate while providing financing for "subprime" borrowers.\par \par \uc2 Generally the funding is relatively short-term, which makes the business highly sensitive to liquidity conditions. Shadow lenders require inflows of new money to pay off maturing obligations. These typically come either directly from the banks for example, via their "wealth management products" or from entities with access to bank financing such as state-owned enterprises.\par \par \uc2 There may be a larger political game going on. \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  is not an independent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so the order to turn off the credit spigot must have come directly from the Politburo. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's surprise attack on bank credit must therefore be understood in the context of the leadership's current focus on improving economic efficiency. This objective will be impossible to achieve unless the central government can overcome resistance from the powerful local interests that benefit from the status quo. The Politburo's goal may have been to starve opponents of reform into submission.\par \par \uc2 Local governments appear to be the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's real targets, because they rely heavily on shadow financing to subvert Beijing's reform initiatives. Shadow funds flow directly into local government projects that the central government views as wasteful, and the funds benefit localities indirectly by pushing up land prices. As long as this money keeps flowing, over-investment in infrastructure, heavy industry and real estate will continue unchecked and Beijing's vision of a new economy driven mainly by consumer demand and productivity growth will be impossible to realize.\par \par \uc2 There are two problems with this approach. First, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy will result in considerable collateral damage. Small- and medium-size private firms will be particularly hard hit. They tend to be ineligible for bank loans and often depend on shadow financing to make ends meet.\par \par \uc2 Second, attacking anti-reform factions will not be enough to generate real reform. Without radical changes in the economic role of local governments, they will quickly return to business as usual once the fallout has cleared.\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  may have won a battle, but the Politburo is far from winning the war.\par \par \uc2 Mr. DeWeaver manages the emerging markets fund Quantrarian Asia Hedge and is the author of "Animal Spirits with Chinese Characteristics" (Palgrave Macmillan, 2012).\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020130709e97900a6p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
