\par\par \b \uc2 Distress Signs Test Beijing's Resolve\b0\par\par\uc2 By Richard Silk in Beijing and Shen Hong in Shanghai   \par \uc2 1,254 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juni 2013\par 07:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Beijing's determination to ride out increasing economic headwinds without resorting to its usual \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  is heightening anxiety in markets around the world.\par \par \uc2 Concern that leaders are offering neither stimulus nor other ways to address mounting evidence of economic weakness grew after a preliminary gauge suggested that China's crucial manufacturing sector contracted faster in June than in May.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, a shortage of cash has intensified this week, with sudden heavy selling in the bond market and companies turning to Hong Kong for funds. Early Friday, rates in China's money markets fell sharply on rumors that Beijing had ordered its big banks to loosen up cash. Still, they remain more than double than average for the year, and the turbulence suggests continued uncertainty in the market for cash in coming days.\par \par \uc2 The squeeze stems in part from skepticism over China's growth prospects, which has scared away some foreign capital, though it is also part of a global flow of cash out of emerging markets as the U.S. Federal Reserve hints it wants to exit quantitative easing.\par \par \uc2 The double whammy of China's deepening signs of distress and the Fed's move combined to hit global equity, bond and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. The pain was particularly acute in emerging markets--extra dependent on selling to China--and coping with the reverse of a flood of capital that had come their way as investors fled the very low interest rates in the U.S. The money now flowing out is weakening both their stock prices and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In China, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could easily step in more directly to add cash and ease the pain, but it has held off so far. Analysts say the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  is sending a signal it won't take the chance of encouraging risky or excessive lending.\par \par \uc2 The turmoil, though, is spreading, with the sharp selloff in the bond market pushing yields on short-term bonds to above those on longer-dated debt, a rare anomaly known as yield-curve inversion. While economists traditionally view an inverted yield curve as a sign of a looming recession--as it is traditionally caused by investors fleeing to safer long-term bonds--the development in China is driven by the cash shortages and heavier selling of short-term paper. A move by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  would easily bring down short-term yields in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's bond markets, which are by and large closed to foreigners.\par \par \uc2 Short-term interest rates have also been surging. An isolated deal Thursday saw the interbank benchmark for funding costs, called the seven-day repo rate, jump as high as 28%.\par \par \uc2 Both the financial-market stress and the recent raft of disappointing economic data present a challenge to new leaders in Beijing who have suggested they are committed to reining in lending and overhauling China's economy--even if that means slower growth in the short term. Past stimulus measures have left a legacy of high leverage, fueling worries about bad debt, as well as severe overcapacity in sectors like steel and cement.\par \par \uc2 "Senior leaders have in recent weeks reconfirmed their intention to work on economic reform instead of stimulus, and the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's policy stance amid the tightness of liquidity on interbank markets is in line with that paradigm," said Louis Kuijs, chief China economist at RBS.\par \par \uc2 With funding pressures threatening to spread to other parts of the economy, the costs of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to stand pat are becoming increasingly evident.\par \par \uc2 "Because of the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's refusal to inject liquidity, interbank funding costs will likely continue to rise in the short run," said Li Zhiqiang, chief economist at \uc2 China Minsheng Banking Corp\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On Friday, rates dropped sharply as traders cited rumors that the central banks told China's biggest state-controlled lenders that they should free up cash for the rest of the system.\par \par \uc2 That drove the benchmark seven-day repurchase-agreement rate, a benchmark for interbank borrowing costs, to 8.33% on a weighted-average basis, down from 11.62% at Thursday's close, but up from 6.90% a week ago. Still, rates remain stubbornly high compared with the steady rate of around 3.3% prior to June.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  didn't immediately respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Those surging rates are also causing problems for companies, with one senior executive at a copper cable maker saying his borrowing costs set by the banks had risen to as high as 8%, from about 4% normally, adding that "if it keeps going up, we'll be in big trouble."\par \par \uc2 The shortage of liquidity started at the end of last month as foreign-fund inflows slowed and banks needed to meet regular capital-requirement needs, but it may also signal ill-disciplined lenders have taken on too much leverage. The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  has ample reserves to ease the pain if it wants to but its intention is to rein in wayward financing behavior and prevent long-term risks in the financial system, analysts say. Some of the concern has focused on lightly regulated "shadow banking."\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the growth concerns hit investors already worried about the signs the Fed may pull back on its efforts to support the U.S. economy. In early trading Friday, China's benchmark Shanghai Composite stock index opened sharply lower but was down 0.9% at midday. Earlier, markets in the U.S. and Europe also dropped.\par \par \uc2 The slowing growth also comes as China's attractiveness as a manufacturing base is weakening. In the first five months of this year, foreign direct investment into China has been near flat, rising only 1.03% compared with a year ago, and in 2012 it fell year-to-year, according to official data.\par \par \uc2 "Over the last five years or so stabilizing growth was the priority," said Qu Hongbin, an economist at \uc2 HSBC\uc2 . "Whenever there was a slowdown they came out with some policy to make sure growth would remain stable. But it's become clear that the new administration prefers reform to stimulus."\par \par \uc2 Still, a stalling economy will tempt the government to ease conditions. Growth dropped to 7.7% in the first quarter, and many economists expect it to fall further in the second quarter. Authorities responded to a similar slowdown last year by cutting interest rates and speeding up infrastructure projects.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, China's State Council, or cabinet, pledged to maintain a "prudent" monetary policy and guard against systemic risks, and reiterated its goal of establishing market-determined interest rates. The council also vowed to halt the flow of credit to industries with overcapacity.\par \par \uc2 "With the economic data disappointing and inflation low, many in the market had expected the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  to keep liquidity flowing," said Wang Tao, an economist at \uc2 UBS\uc2 , but "the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  has made it clear in the past 10 days that overly rapid credit expansion would not be accommodated."\par \par \uc2 The preliminary \uc2 HSBC\uc2  PMI, a nationwide survey of manufacturing activity, fell to 48.3 in June from 49.2 in May, its lowest level in nine months, with anything less than 50 indicating contraction.\par \par \uc2 "This kind of data will test the resolve of the government to maintain its current macro policy stance," said Mr. Kuijs of RBS.\par \par \uc2 Write to Richard Silk at richard.silk@dowjones.com and Shen Hong at hong.shen@dowjones.com [ 21-06-13 0520GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130621e96l00041\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
