\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 1-Zloty slips post- \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 ,  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2  data, Fed doubts weigh\b0\par\par\uc2 699 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juni 2013\par 10:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 By Jason Hovet PRAGUE, June 10 (Reuters) - The zloty fell on Monday, after jumping late last week when the \uc2 Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped in to help the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with weak data out of China and doubts over the U.S. Fed's bond-buying programme hurting central European assets. Emerging markets have been prey over the last month to worries over how soon the Federal Reserve will begin reducing its programme of stimulus for the U.S. economy. "Emerging markets are weakening; it is probably due to worries over the slowdown in Chinese growth. The shakeout from emerging markets is continuing," a Budapest-based dealer said. "The prospect of a cutback in the Fed's money printing \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  and general risk aversion as well as a continued rise in European yields can keep up pressure on emerging markets." The \uc2 Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen buying zlotys on Friday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a one-year low of 4.327 to the euro last week. By 0817 GMT, the zloty had lost 0.5 percent to 4.255.\par \par \uc2  Dealers said the zloty was now moving to a neutral point after gaining as much as 2 percent on Friday. "As governor (Marek) Belka said, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not try to reverse the trend but to counteract the volatility and hit speculators over the knuckles," a Warsaw-based dealer said. "The zloty fell too quickly and this move was very pronounced, which was not to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's liking." Other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  also fell, with the Hungarian forint down 0.8 percent and Romania's leu off 0.5 percent. The Czech crown was steady at 25.535 to the euro, holding on to a 0.6 percent rise since mid-May thanks to its safe-haven status in central Europe. But dealers say it is nearing a correction, especially as data showing the economy remains deep in recession do not back up its recent rise. "Some buyers can come, at least some profit taking can happen. I think this 25.500 level can be some short-term bottom (for EUR/CZK)," said Raiffeisenbank dealer Ivo Prokop. Stock markets in the region were also able to gain some ground, led by a 0.9 percent gain for Budapest.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                   CEE MARKETS SNAPSHOT AT 1018 CET\par  ************************** \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ************************\par                              Latest  Previous   Daily   Change\par                              bid     close      change  in 2013\par  Czech crown                 25.535    25.534   0.00%  -1.91%\par  Hungarian forint           297.310   294.860   -0.82%  -2.14%\par  Polish zloty                 4.255     4.234    -0.48%  -4.24%\par  Romanian leu                 4.536     4.515   -0.46%   -2.05%\par  Croatian kuna                7.510     7.520   +0.14% +0.55%\par  Serbian dinar              114.170   114.280   +0.10%   -1.64%\par  Note: daily change calculated from previous close at 1600 GMT\par  **************************** STOCKS **************************\par                              Latest  Previous   Daily   Change\par                                      close      change  in 2013\par  Prague                      955.81    957.45   -0.17%  -7.98%\par  Budapest                  19791.18  19632.63   +0.81%  +8.90%\par  Warsaw                     2506.55   2488.84   +0.71%  -2.96%\par  Bucharest                  5395.26   5372.56  +0.42%  +4.77%\p