\par\par \b \uc2 DJ  Treasury's Lew: Concern Over China Yuan Policy 'Not Over at All'\b0\par\par\uc2 538 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juni 2013\par 00:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Newswires English Content\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 By Michael J. Casey\par \par \uc2 U.S. concerns over China's management of its exchange rate are "not over at all" as an issue between the two countries, despite a steady, three-year increase in the Chinese yuan versus the dollar, said Treasury Secretary \uc2 Jacob Lew\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mr. Lew's comments, made during a wide-ranging interview at The Wall Street Journal, come as Chinese President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  and President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  prepare to meet later Friday, their first face-to-face meeting since Mr. Xi was sworn in three months ago.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Lew said Chinese officials are acutely aware of how much the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  problem matters to the U.S. "It was central to all the meetings I had," he said of a visit he made to China in March. "This issue is not going away and they know it is not going away."\par \par \uc2 In a twice-yearly report to Congress in April, the Treasury Department said China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains "significantly undervalued." However, it again declined to label the government in Beijing as a "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator," a status that would have opened the door to possible trade sanctions.\par \par \uc2 And in a mark of the continued lobbying by American businesses that complain of unfair Chinese competition, a bipartisan group of senators introduced legislation Wednesday aimed at giving the U.S. new tools to fight \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange rate policies such as \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  currently allows the yuan to fluctuate within an exchange rate band set around a fixed midpoint value versus the dollar. Over time since 2010, the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  has gradually, and in fits and starts, increased the midpoint value of the yuan while modestly widening the trading band. It also liberalized yuan trading by opening up offshore trading centers in Hong Kong and other cities.\par \par \uc2 However, the government continues to tightly manage the flow of funds into and out of the yuan with capital controls that limit \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  transactions outside of those for commercial trade purposes.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Lew said that on this and other contentious issues within the bilateral relationship, including accusations that Chinese state-owned companies have hacked into U.S. companies' databases, "the basic lever we have is that they very much want a different relationship going forward." He said China wants "a seat at the table where countries establish the rules of the international economy. That means they also have to live by the rules."\par \par \uc2 He added that China seems reluctant to embrace the economic disruption that would come with full-scale economic reform, including the liberalization of state-owned companies.\par \par \uc2 "They say, 'we don't want to create Detroits all over China'," he said. "My response to that is you don't want to be Detroit but you do want to be Pittsburgh...You don't want to be stuck in a place from which you can't come back, and you do want to be able to ride the wave of the future and not the past."\par \par \uc2 --Brenda Cronin contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Michael J. Casey at michael.j.casey@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 07-06-13 2255GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020130607e967000n3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
