\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 1-China opens new front in war as yuan speculation distorts export data\b0\par\par\uc2 1,075 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Mai 2013\par 12:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Economists suspect trade data conceals speculative inflows\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 PBOC\uc2  signals it will intensify mop up of speculative funds\par \par \uc2 * C.bank to issue 3-month bills for first time since 2011\par \par \uc2 * Investors trying to cash in on long-running yuan rally\par \par \uc2 * Regulators worry money not being routed into real economy\par \par \uc2 By Pete Sweeney\par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, May 8 (Reuters) - \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  signalled on Wednesday it was prepared to change its monetary strategy to fend off inflows of speculative capital, as Beijing struggles to control a tide of cash washing in from overseas markets.\par \par \uc2 The move came as April exports blew past expectations, which appeared on the surface to indicate that both China's economy and global demand were on the mend. But economists were quick to suspect the figures were artificially inflated by investors who were disguising speculative bets on the yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as trade payments.\par \par \uc2 Faced with the risk that such inflows could cause the yuan to appreciate so quickly that it destabilises exports and the broader economy, the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  (PBOC) \uc2 has begun \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market this year, buying up dollars and selling yuan.\par \par \uc2 This leaves the question of how to keep the yuan it has sold from distorting domestic markets.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday morning, during a routine call to primary dealers in China's interbank market, dealers told Reuters that regulators had queried them for demand for three-month bills.\par \par \uc2 Hours later, after markets had closed, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  said it would auction 10 billion yuan ($1.6 billion) of three-month bills on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 While a relatively small amount, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not issued bills - which drain liquidity in tenors between three months and three years - since 2011. It has instead relied on short-tenor bond repurchase agreements to move money rapidly in and out of the market, much as central banks in developed economies do.\par \par \uc2 The move suggests that the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  is preparing to make systematic longer-term cash drains from the money supply to blunt the impact of hot money flows, with a potential knock-on impact on interest rates, market sentiment and economic growth.\par \par \uc2 The announcement came days after the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulator released new rules to crack down on inflows of hot money disguised as trade payments.\par \par \uc2 Calls to the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  for comment were not answered.\par \par \uc2 The last time the government made technical adjustments to its money management methods in February, domestic equity markets swooned as investors bailed out of stocks, worried that the changes heralded a wider monetary tightening that would constrict growth.\par \par \uc2 But the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  must balance the risk that the change will negatively impact markets against the distorting effect the inflows are having on the wider economy, in particular on exports, and by signs that capital is once again being misrouted internally into speculative channels like property instead of the real economy.\par \par \uc2 A Reuters estimate of hot money flows based on official data indicates that as much as $181 billion in speculative cash may have entered China in the first quarter, fueled in part by loose monetary policy from the United States and Europe. (GRAPHIC:{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/raz74t"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/raz74t}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 And that estimate likely understates the true figure, since it doesn't account for inflows disguised as trade payments.\par \par \uc2 These inflows have helped push the yuan to new highs against the dollar on a near-daily basis, attracting even more speculation. On Wednesday, it closed at a record 6.1410 per dollar, up 1.5 percent for the year so far after rising slightly over 1 percent in 2012.\par \par \uc2 POLICY HEADACHE\par \par \uc2 While China's capital account is tightly restricted, it is not hermetically sealed, and some of the speculative inflows are conducted through legal channels.\par \par \uc2 But there are two major points of concern. One is that domestic trading companies are betting too much on further yuan appreciation, to the extent that they are borrowing the dollars they need for trade with foreign companies in order to increase their yuan stockpiles, which most economists see as unsustainable.\par \par \uc2 The other is that companies are falsifying trade transactions to get their hands on even more yuan, muddying macroeconomic indicators and embarrassing regulators.\par \par \uc2 Official data showed that April exports grew by 14.7 percent from a year earlier, well above expectations of 10.3 percent, but the numbers only fueled scepticism that financial manoeuvring by exporters and speculative inflows are masking weakness in real demand.\par \par \uc2 Particularly suspicious were figures in both March and April showing explosive growth in exports to Hong Kong and bonded trading zones, that appeared completely disconnected from export growth to ultimate destination markets in the U.S. and Europe - and from tepid export figures for neighboring Asian economies.\par \par \uc2 Louis Kuijs and Tiffany Qiu, economists at RBS in Hong Kong, estimated that actual April export growth was only 5.7 percent, implying that the other 9 percent was actually comprised of falsified invoices created to allow trading companies to increase their yuan holdings.\par \par \uc2 "It's creating a big headache for policymakers," said Wei Yao, economist at \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DON'T PANIC\par \par \uc2 However, interbank dealers who spoke to Reuters did not expect a resumption of bill issuance to panic the markets this time around, as investors are well aware of the hot money issue after repeated regulatory warnings and crackdowns.\par \par \uc2 Both equity and money markets were relatively bullish on Wednesday, and money rates eased on expectations that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will actually allow more funds, not less, to flow into the market through scheduled open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Liu Junyu, a bond and money market analyst at \uc2 China Merchants Bank\uc2  in Shenzhen, said that the market did not read the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's move as a portent of more drastic tightening measures like interest rate hikes or increases to reserve requirement ratios at banks, both of which would make more profound and durable adjustments to base money supply that might choke off China's economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 "Given so much weak economic data and recent dubious trade data, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  will probably keep monetary policy unchanged and neutral," he said.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Kevin Yao in BEIJING and Li Hongwei, Lu Jianxin, Gabriel Wildau in SHANGHAI; Editing by Kim Coghill)\par \par \uc2 CHINA-ECONOMY/ (WRAPUP 1, PIX) | C | D | E | M | O | U | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL | G | RBN | ABX | BNX | FUN | Z | RNP | DNP | PGE   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130508e958000j1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
