\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-ANALYSIS-Chinese strong talk on yuan reform masks policy quandary\b0\par\par\uc2 993 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 April 2013\par 23:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Repeats story first carried on Saturday)\par \par \uc2 * Chinese regulators voice commitment to reform\par \par \uc2 * At same time, \uc2 PBOC\uc2  increases market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Government balancing liberalisation versus stability\par \par \uc2 By Pete Sweeney and Lu Jianxin\par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, April 27 (Reuters) - \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been talking tough on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reform while it has also intensified market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , highlighting the fine line it must walk in trying to liberalise the yuan.\par \par \uc2 Critics, including the United States, see the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as another sign that Beijing is dragging its feet in letting market forces determine the yuan's exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 But the wall of money being printed by China's trading partners under their super-loose monetary policies is flooding emerging markets with speculative cash, which has put the yuan under strong upward pressure this year.\par \par \uc2 "It's difficult to make the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation argument," said Zhang Zhiwei, chief China economist for Nomura Securities in Hong Kong. "There are a lot of things that central bankers are doing nowadays that are not really regarded as best practice; we are in uncharted waters these days and everything is relative."\par \par \uc2 The yuan is a closely managed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that is allowed to trade 1 percent either side of a midpoint set by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  each day. However, Beijing is steadily relaxing its tight grip on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as it tries to restructure the world's second-biggest economy away from a reliance on exports and towards greater domestic consumption.\par \par \uc2 Critics, including many Western governments, argue that Beijing is not opening its markets quickly enough and some suggest moves to date have lacked any real substance. For its part, China fears that opening up too fast will destabilise the economy.\par \par \uc2 Many dealers thought the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was signalling a loosening of its control of the market in the fourth quarter of 2012 when it largely abstained from its traditional role of buying up \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Even when companies refused to exchange dollars for yuan within the daily permitted trading band, prompting a collapse in trading volumes, the regulator stayed on the sidelines.\par \par \uc2 But 2013 has seen the return of a nanny \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the amount of dollars entering the economy picked up. China's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, already the world's largest by far, swelled by $128 billion in the first quarter, the biggest quarterly increase since the second quarter of 2011.\par \par \uc2 "Recent resumption of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a large scale is troubling," the \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  said in a semi-annual \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  report issued on April 12.\par \par \uc2 The report argued that China was once again keeping the yuan at an artificially low level by buying up foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It also criticised \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's unwillingness to disclose its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a transparent manner.\par \par \uc2 As the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  (PBOC) \uc2 was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , central bankers suggested \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained firmly on the path of reform.\par \par \uc2 Deputy governor \uc2 Yi Gang\uc2  told an \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  forum in Washington on April 17 that the time had come to widen the yuan's trading band again. A day later, he said conditions were ripe to further open up China's capital account.\par \par \uc2 Yi's statements followed similar comments by a more junior \uc2 PBOC\uc2  official that were given prominent play in the official English-language China Daily.\par \par \uc2 The comments received less coverage in Chinese-language state media, which some dealers said suggested that the reform talk was political gesturing for foreign consumption.\par \par \uc2 GENUINELY BULLISH\par \par \uc2 Adding to the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  criticism, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  set its daily midpoint at a series of record highs in the run-up to the G20 meeting in Washington late last week, prompting speculation it was pushing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher to head off potential criticism that it was undervalued.\par \par \uc2 "The slew of record-high midpoints are contrary to the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's repeated pledge to keep the yuan stable," said a trader at a European bank in Shanghai. "Given this lack of transparency, lots of speculative talk is circulating."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  let the yuan appreciate 2.36 basis points between April 12 to 17, setting record highs along the way, compared with a rise of only 1.54 basis points for all of 2012.\par \par \uc2 Those dates spanned the publication of the Treasury \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  report, a visit to China by U.S. Secretary of State John Kerry and the G20 meeting - events that have served as platforms for criticism of China \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls in the past.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, the yuan has continued to set record highs since the end of the summit, and \uc2 PBOC\uc2  data shows that corporates, both onshore and off, remain genuinely bullish on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WHAT FOREIGNERS WANT\par \par \uc2 Another quandary for Beijing is that liberalisation of the yuan, while economically beneficial in the long run, could impede in the near term the promotion of the usage of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in international trade.\par \par \uc2 Getting trading partners to invoice transactions in yuan would reduce exchange rate risk for Chinese firms and reduce the need to continue accumulating foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, which were equivalent to about 40 percent of China's GDP in 2012.\par \par \uc2 But profit-seeking companies are less interested in helping China reduce exchange rate risk than helping their own bottom lines, and when the yuan fell against the dollar in the first half of 2012 as the Greek credit crisis roiled global markets, companies backed off from holding yuan assets or settling trade in yuan.\par \par \uc2 "It's good to maintain at least a stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  with some small appreciation," said Patrick Wu, head of yuan trading at \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Today the renminbi obviously is outperforming. The reason is they want to show a good gesture to corporates saying, 'Come here, use our \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for settlement'."\par \par \uc2 This is not the same as liberalisation, he said, but for the time being it is necessary.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Neil Fullick)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-CHINA/YUAN (ANALYSIS, REPEAT) | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | ABX | BNX | FUN | Z  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130501e94s000pi\par 