\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ China's  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  to Loosen Control of Yuan, Part of Plan to Free Up Usage of Its  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 373 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 April 2013\par 00:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 17 Apr 2013 19:07 EDT DJ  China's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to Loosen Control of Yuan - PBOC Vice Governor\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Natasha Brereton-Fukui \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON--\uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  plans to loosen the mechanism it uses to control the yuan in the near future, part of plans to free up the usage of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the next couple of years, \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  Vice Governor Yi Gang said Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 "The exchange rate is going to be more market oriented," Mr. Yi said on a panel at the International Monetary Fund Spring Meetings in Washington. "I think in the near future we are going to increase the floating band even further."\par \par \uc2 The PBOC last April doubled the yuan's trading band to 1.0% on either side of its daily reference rate.\par \par \uc2 Speaking to reporters later, Mr. Yi said market conditions made it "appropriate" to consider increasing the trading band again and doing so would be good for the market.\par \par \uc2 "I think one [certainty is that] the direction is clear, and the other [certainty] is we're going to increase the floating band," Mr. Yi said. "I should emphasize that we will do it in a gradual manner, such it won't cause instable effects."\par \par \uc2 The PBOC official also pledged to gradually ease restrictions on the usage of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  going forward.\par \par \uc2 "We will steadily push the capital-account convertibility reform further," Mr. Yi said. "So you will see that maybe in the next couple of years, China step by step will gradually go along that direction."\par \par \uc2 Also on Wednesday, U.S. Treasury Secretary Jacob Lew reiterated particular concerns raised in his department's semiannual \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  report late last week about \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent increased \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep a lid on the yuan's value.\par \par \uc2 "We are concerned that in recent months, movement towards more \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility appears to have slowed and the pace of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market has picked up," the secretary said.\par \par \uc2 Write to Natasha Brereton-Fukui at natasha.brereton@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 17, 2013 19:07 ET (23:07 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130417e94h005z0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
