\par\par \b \uc2 DJ China Yuan up Late on \uc2 PBOC\uc2  Guidance; Highest Close Under Current System -2-\b0\par\par\uc2 463 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 April 2013\par 11:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Newswires English Content\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                                   Vs Parity  Previous \par USD/CNY Central Parity   6.2408               6.2454 \par USD/CNY OTC 0830 GMT     6.1831     -0.92%    6.1871 \par             High         6.1872     -0.86% \par             Low          6.1798     -0.98% \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SHANGHAI--China's yuan rose against the U.S. dollar late on Tuesday tracking the record-high reference rate set by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 Traders expect the yuan to remain firm owing to less government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On the over-the-counter market the dollar was at CNY6.1831 around 0830 GMT--down from Monday's close of CNY6.1871. It traded between CNY6.1798--its lowest since the launch of the modern Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading system in 1994--and CNY6.1872.\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  set the dollar/yuan central parity rate at 6.2408--lower than Tuesday's 6.2454--creating another fresh high for the yuan fixing.\par \par \uc2 "The recent declines in the dollar/yuan central parity rate indicate less \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  heightening expectations for a stronger yuan among companies. So, they are rushing to convert the dollar into the Chinese yuan," a Shanghai-based trader with a local bank said.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. said in its semiannual report on world-wide exchange rates on Friday that Beijing has substantially reduced its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the yuan allowing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to appreciate 10.0% against the dollar since June 2010. Washington also said "further appreciation" of the yuan is warranted.\par \par \uc2 "While the first-quarter GDP data are disappointing it's still widely expected that a mild recovery in China is likely this year. As long as the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  refrains from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  the yuan will post a further rise in its value," the Shanghai-based trader said.\par \par \uc2 China's first quarter gross domestic product growth came in at an unexpectedly weak 7.7% on year--below the 7.9% on-year rise in the fourth quarter of last year and short of expectations of 8%, according to a poll of economists by Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 Another Shanghai-based trader with a foreign bank said she put support for the U.S. unit at CNY6.1600. The yuan has risen 0.8% since the start of this year.\par \par \uc2 Offshore, one-year dollar/yuan nondeliverable forward contracts rose to 6.2551/6.2588 from 6.2535/6.2565 late on Monday implying a 1.2% fall by the yuan over the next year.\par \par \uc2 In the offshore yuan market in Hong Kong, where the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  floats freely, the dollar was at CNY6.1862 late on Tuesday--higher than CNY6.1855 late Monday.\par \par \uc2 Write to Rose Yu at rose.yu@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 16-04-13 0931GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020130416e94g000go\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
