\par\par \b \uc2 FACTBOX-Key political risks to watch in the Philippines\b0\par\par\uc2 By Manuel Mogato   \par \uc2 1,336 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 April 2013\par 06:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 MANILA, April 11 (Reuters) - The government and the country's largest Muslim rebel group have agreed a preliminary deal that may end a 40-year conflict that has killed more than 120,000 and displaced 2 million.\par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Benigno Aquino\uc2  hopes this will pave the way for economic growth and development in the south, a poor region but one that sits on considerable resources.\par \par \uc2 In March it won its first investment-grade credit rating when Fitch upgraded it, coming after \uc2 Moody's\uc2  raised its rank for the country last October and \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  did in July.\par \par \uc2 In 2012, the economy grew 6.6 percent, beating expectations and putting its rate of expansion second only to China.\par \par \uc2 Still, much of the country is dilapidated and vulnerable to natural disaster. Typhoon Bopha killed thousands in the south late last year, and heavy rains in August swamped huge swathes of Manila, killing more than 100 people and leaving hundreds of thousands homeless.\par \par \uc2 Internationally, by far the greatest concern is the South China Sea, contested waters where the U.S.-backed Philippines are increasingly running up against China.\par \par \uc2 RATINGS: (Unchanged unless stated:)\par \par \uc2 S&P: BB+\par \par \uc2 MOODY'S: Ba1\par \par \uc2 FITCH: BBB- (Upgraded from BB+ on March 27)\par \par \uc2 Following is a summary of political risks to watch:\par \par \uc2 INTERNAL SECURITY\par \par \uc2 Negotiators from the government and the Muslim rebel group Moro Islamic Liberation Front (MILF) have reached a deal on a roadmap to set up a new autonomous Bangsamoro region in the south of the mainly Roman Catholic state, signalling an end to a long insurgency.\par \par \uc2 Still, the 11,000-member MILF is not the only active rebel group. Maoist guerrillas have attacked private mining projects on southern island Mindanao, destroying around $70 million worth of equipment, and threatening more attacks. A small al Qaeda-linked Islamist militant group also remains active on remote islands in the southern Philippines, kidnapping foreigners and local traders.\par \par \uc2 Overall, internal security remains weak, persistently highlighted by foreign embassies in travel advisories, with law enforcement hobbled by corruption, lack of police resources, and easy availability of guns on the street.\par \par \uc2 What to watch:\par \par \uc2 - Implementation of the political deal to set up an autonomous region in Muslim-dominated areas in the south. This will include the demobilisation, disarmament and reintegration of former Muslim rebel fighters, imposition of local taxes to cut government subsidies, and revenue-sharing arrangements with oil and gas producers.\par \par \uc2 - Laws drafted by a 15-member panel of government and rebel representatives that will create a new autonomous government for that region.\par \par \uc2 - Any more attacks on mines or other businesses, and how investors respond. The Philippine army has said it lacks the resources to contain attacks, so has asked firms to hire private militias to guard their businesses.\par \par \uc2 - Diplomatic fallout from the peace talks with Muslim rebels after the incursion of 200 Filipinos, belonging to an ancient sultanate, on Malaysia's eastern state of Sabah, which resulted in a bloody confrontation in early 2013.\par \par \uc2 SOUTH CHINA SEA\par \par \uc2 The Philippines is at the centre of Asia's most likely military flashpoint: the South China Sea. Last year a regional summit failed to agree a position on the competing claims of various nations, and what appears to be the increasing alignment of Washington with Manila risks infuriating \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In January the Philippines asked an international tribunal to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its longstanding dispute with \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  and declare that Beijing's claims are invalid, a move China rejected.\par \par \uc2 That came soon after U.S. and Philippine officials agreed on an increase in the number of U.S. military ships, aircraft and troops rotating through the Philippines, though officials from both countries say there is no plan to revive permanent U.S. military bases there.\par \par \uc2 Manila will not surrender claims to its exclusive economic zone, as defined by the United Nations, but it cannot hope to confront China militarily.\par \par \uc2 Beijing wants one-one-negotiations, but Manila and other claimants prefer a multilateral approach, which opens the way for an indirect role for the United States. China wants the United States to stay out of the dispute.\par \par \uc2 What to watch:\par \par \uc2 - New security arrangements between Washington and Manila to increase the U.S. military footprint in the Philippines, the U.S. fighter jets and warships the Philippines is able to buy, and how China responds to any Filipino military buildup.\par \par \uc2 - Any action taken by the tribunal of the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea (UNCLOS) on Manila's request for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 - Fresh approaches by Manila to pursue its claims on the disputed Spratly Islands. Aquino has said Manila is looking into at least five other options to pursue its claims after \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  rejected arbitration, including asking for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the International Tribunal on the Law of the Sea (ITLOS) in Germany.\par \par \uc2 - Commercial activity in the South China Sea. Manila has accepted exploration bids on two oil and gas in the disputed areas, and an Anglo-Filipino company may start drilling oil wells later this year in the Reed Bank, another area claimed by China. Spending on upgrades of air and naval equipment, including radar stations. The Philippines plans to roll out $1.8 billion in defence spending in the next four years, but says its actions are not aggressive.\par \par \uc2 ECONOMIC GROWTH; CHURCH V STATE?\par \par \uc2 Fitch's credit rating upgrade is a vote of confidence in the government's efforts curb corruption and increase infrastructure spending, and comes as more and more economies in the West struggle to avoid credit rating downgrades. The move is likely to attract higher levels of investment.\par \par \uc2 The government has lifted an 18-month moratorium on new resources exploration projects, but commercial \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not start until lawmakers approve new legislation on mineral revenues and a presidential executive order is signed.\par \par \uc2 Congressional approval is likely to hold up permits further, stalling up to $12 billion in new investments planned over the next five years, including Southeast Asia's biggest undeveloped copper-gold mine, the $5.9 billion Tampakan project by global miner Xstrata Plc and Australia's \uc2 Indophil Resources NL\par \par \uc2 in the south of the country.\par \par \uc2 Still, the economy is performing well. With 6.6 percent growth in 2012, the Philippines is an example of expansion in contrast to Europe and the United States.\par \par \uc2 Authorities are struggling to control an inflow of capital that has pushed up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and threatens asset price bubbles, and government is concerned about the impact of a strong peso on the competitiveness of its exports, remittances from Filipinos abroad and the growing outsourcing sector.\par \par \uc2 Separately, Congress approved in December a highly controversial reproductive health bill, requiring the government to hand out contraceptives to poor people, a policy bitterly opposed by the Catholic church. The move, the first time in more than a decade an elected politician has taken on the bishops and won, was a sign of Aquino's confidence and his bedrock of national support, but is unlikely to be the last battle between church and state.\par \par \uc2 What to watch:\par \par \uc2 - Whether other ratings agencies follow Fitch in upgrading the Philippines to investment-level ranks.\par \par \uc2 - Progress on the government's big-ticket infrastructure projects to spur investment, create jobs and further lift the economy.\par \par \uc2 - How quickly mining firms start work on new projects. [20130411 044911 GMT]\par \par \uc2 - Growth figures, and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy moves.\par \par \uc2 - Political fallout from Aquino's challenge to the influential Roman Catholic bishops in pushing reform of contraception laws as his administration faces mid-term elections in May.\par \par \uc2 - A Supreme Court ruling on the reproductive health law after Catholic lay groups won a temporary restraining order on the law's implementation.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Daniel Magnowski) [20130411 044911 GMT]\par \par \uc2 PHILIPPINES-RISKS/ (FACTBOX) | ABN | E | RBN | UKI | AUF | PHD | G | AFA | CSA | LBY | RWSA | RWS | REULB | GNS | RNA | M | SXNA|PHILIPPINES-RISKS/ (FACTBOX)=2 | ABN | E | RBN | UKI | AUF | PHD | G | AFA | CSA | LBY | RWSA | RWS | REULB | GNS | RNA | M | SXNA|chained   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130411e94b00056\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 No Reason to Think the Dollar's Recent Rally Is Over\b0\par\par\uc2 863 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2013\par 06:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones Korean Newswires English Content\par \uc2 DJNKO\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2     --Safe havens are in demand as global sentiment takes another dive \par \par \uc2       --Other central banks will likely be more dovish than the U.S. Fed \par \par \uc2       --Even the latest Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will work in the dollar's favor \par \par \uc2       By Nicholas Hastings \par \par \uc2       The dollar has been going from strength to strength. \par \par \uc2       And there is little reason why this should change. \par \par \uc2       There may be fears that, after a strong end to last year, U.S. growth is starting to lose momentum and the Federal Reserve will have to maintain a dovish stance. \par \par \uc2       But, with global growth concerns remaining high and the euro-zone crisis once again looming large, there is little reason for investors to find the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  any less attractive than before. \par \par \uc2       Since early February, the dollar index has managed to rise by 5% but its ascent has slowed down recently as economic data have proved a little less robust than expected. \par \par \uc2       Although there are still some Fed members calling for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to start reducing its $85 billion a month bond-buying program sooner rather than later, the majority, including Fed chairman Ben Bernanke, are sticking to the dovish line that the purchases should continue at this rate through the end of the year. \par \par \uc2       Boston Fed President Eric Rosengren summed it up with his comment late Thursday that the benefits of this policy outweigh the costs for now. \par \par \uc2       This has all helped to cap a recent rise in \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields, with the 10-year yield falling back to 1.85% after a rally to just over 2% last month. \par \par \uc2       For the dollar, however, events outside the U.S. matter just as much, if not more. \par \par \uc2       Global sentiment has taken a fresh knock in recent weeks. Renewed worries about the euro-zone crisis, a further slowdown in euro-zone growth, promises of more aggressive monetary policy easing in Japan and some uncertainty over the pace of growth in China have all helped to drive investors into safe havens such as the dollar. \par \par \uc2       Much of this could come to a head next week with a series of key \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meetings as well as the likely delayed reaction in the euro to the latest twists of the debt crisis. \par \par \uc2       As Cyprus becomes the first euro-zone member to employ \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls and the country reopens its banks, there is considerable concern that the problems of the tiny island could yet have a wider impact. \par \par \uc2       The political problems in Italy have also come to the fore now that attempts by Pier Luigi Bersani to form a new government have failed. This is likely to put the country back to square one and increase speculation that another election will have to be called. \par \par \uc2       There are already signs that both Cyprus and Italy are starting to drive the yields on some debtor countries higher, a trend that if it accelerates will tip the euro zone back into crisis mode as borrowing costs rise and the need for more sovereign bailouts increases. \par \par \uc2       On top of that there is the problem of euro-zone growth. Data in recent weeks point to a marked slowdown in Germany, and even in Switzerland, suggesting that neither of these two are immune to the recessions in the weaker euro-zone countries. \par \par \uc2       The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  itself may well be forced to acknowledge this when it meets next week and hint that it could be considering lower rates. \par \par \uc2       For the dollar, events in Japan are equally important, even if the flows into and out of the yen are less clear. \par \par \uc2       Expectations of more aggressive easing of monetary policy by the Bank of Japan next week could well be disappointed and help to push the yen back up against the dollar, especially given the yen's superior safe-haven status. \par \par \uc2       Nevertheless, the prospect of continued deflation in Japan will depress global growth hopes even more and ensure that the dollar continues to rise against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2       In a similar vein, the lack of certainty about China's growth prospects is also undermining sentiment, with the dollar set to benefit from new data suggesting that Beijing remains keen to keep a lid on yuan strength. \par \par \uc2       The bottom line for the dollar? \par \par \uc2       Growth may not be so hot in the U.S., and the Fed will probably remain dovish. But with growth elsewhere in the world of even more concern and with other central banks likely to turn even more dovish, there is little reason for investors to stop buying the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2       And with \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  seemingly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  again, there is even less reason to do so. \par \par \uc2       (This is an opinion column by Nicholas Hastings, who is a Senior Correspondent in London for Dow Jones Newswires and has written about \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  for more than 20 years. He previously covered a variety of markets, including equities, fixed income, commodities and energy. He can be contacted on +44-20-7842-9493, by email at nick.hastings@dowjones.com or on Twitter @NickHastingsDJ)  [     March 28, 2013 07:40 ET (11:40 GMT)  ] [   (END) Dow Jones Newswires ] [   29-03-13 0513GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1066 1074   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJNKO00020130329e93t0000d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
