\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 CHINA \highlight\uc2  MONEY-PBOC returns to  \highlight22\uc2 intervening \highlight\uc2  amid capital inflows\b0\par\par\uc2 749 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2013\par 04:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  * \uc2 PBOC\uc2 , state banks buy dollars to curb yuan appreciation\par \par \uc2  * Heavy capital inflows flood onshore market with dollars\par \par \uc2  * Decent exports, weak imports add dollar supply\par \par \uc2  * Global woes, China leadership changes call for stable yuan \par \par \uc2  By Lu Jianxin and Gabriel Wildau\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, March 28 (Reuters) - \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says it has let market forces play a greater role in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, but traders suspect that authorities have stepped up their \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year to curb the yuan's ascent amid a worrying surge of capital inflows.\par \par \uc2  Hot money flows have been driven by large-scale quantitative easing by the U.S. Federal Reserve and other Western central banks since the second half of 2012, causing \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  to flood into China's interbank market at a time when regulators are eager to keep the yuan largely stable, traders say. \par \par \uc2  China's trade surplus, however, is still the biggest source of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows. The world's second-largest economy has recorded healthy export growth this year, while a slower-than-expected recovery in domestic demand has crimped imports.\par \par \uc2  Exporters, moreover, are eager to swap the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  they receive through trade into local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Chinese bank clients - mostly corporates involved in foreign trade - bought $32.2 billion more yuan than foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from banks in February, data showed on Monday.\par \par \uc2  That marked the sixth straight month of net yuan purchases by corporates, indicating that yuan demand still outweighs dollar demand onshore. \par \par \uc2  Despite such upward pressure on the yuan, it has only risen a paltry 0.3 percent in 2013.\par \par \uc2  "The yuan has once again been put under tight state control, with little movement recently," said a senior trader at a U.S. bank in Shanghai. \par \par \uc2  "A steep drop in volatility has wiped out any speculative activity in the onshore market, and there is no evidence that trend will end any time soon."\par \par \uc2  Onshore benchmark one-year yuan/dollar implied volatilities\par \par \uc2 have hit a repeated record low of 1.3 percent this month, while their offshore counterparts have dropped to a decade low.\par \par \uc2  Over the last year, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials claimed repeatedly that the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  had reduced its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market because the yuan exchange rate was already close to its equilibrium value.  \par \par \uc2  CAPITAL INFLOWS\par \par \uc2  In order to hold the yuan steady, the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  (PBOC) \uc2 has had to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to mop up the enormous quantity of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  that has flowed into China this year.\par \par \uc2  Chinese banks, including the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , purchased a record-high net 683.7 billion yuan ($110 billion) in January, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed. Though imprecise, bank \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases serve as a rough proxy for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows into China.\par \par \uc2  In the same month, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets rose by 351.51 billion yuan, according to data.\par \par \uc2  Taken together, the figures indicate that the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  was responsible for more than half of all net \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases by Chinese banks in January. That shows how aggressive the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  was about mopping up excess dollars from the interbank market to rein in the yuan.\par \par \uc2  The market is eagerly awaiting data on bank \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases for February. Market participants expect net purchases of around 200 billion yuan - less than January's record high, but still well ahead of the 40 billion yuan monthly average in 2012.\par \par \uc2  Very loose conditions in the money market in recent weeks also support expectations of continued large \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows in February and March. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark seven-day weighted-average repo rate\par \par \uc2 was at 3.17 percent on Thursday, despite a resumption of repo issuance by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at sterilising excess yuan liquidity that enters the banking system when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buys \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Though the trade surplus was responsible for the bulk of the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows, Reuters' estimate of hot money flows based on the bank \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases and other data found that about $80 billion in hot money flowed into China in January, reversing nine consecutive months of outflows. \par \par \uc2  Reflecting the upward pressure, the yuan has stubbornly moved at or near the upper limit of the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's official midpoint since late last year. Had the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  and state banks not continuously bought dollars, traders say the yuan would have long broken through the key 6.20 per dollar resistance.\par \par \uc2 ($1=6.214 Yuan) \par \par \uc2 (Editing by Jacqueline Wong)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-CHINA/DEBT | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | Z | ABX | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130328e93s00045\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
