\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ BLOG: The Steady Hand of China's Central Banker: Eswar Prasad on Zhou Xiaoxhuan:\b0\par\par\uc2 1,349 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2013\par 10:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (This story has been posted on The Wall Street Journal Online's China Real Time Report blog at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/chinarealtime"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/chinarealtime  }}}\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 Fear of inflation and hope for reform in China's monetary policy are both rising. Lesser men might have balked, but \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  governor \uc2 Zhou Xiaochuan\uc2  is facing the challenge.\par \par \uc2 Two months ago, Mr. Zhou was expected to step down after a decade at the helm of \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but banking and Communist Party officials have recently indicated that China's leaders want him to stay on a little longer. During a nationally televised news conference on the sidelines of China's annual parliamentary meetings on Wednesday, Mr. Zhou refused to say whether he would stay or go.\par \par \uc2 China Real Time caught up with Eswar Prasad, an expert on China's economy at Cornell University, and threatened to keep 20% of his bank deposits in low- yielding reserves unless he shared his insights on Mr. Zhou's record.\par \par \uc2 What was the economy that \uc2 Zhou Xiaochuan\uc2  inherited in 2002?\par \par \uc2 When Governor Zhou became the head of the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  in December 2002, the economy was growing fast and recovering from a brief spell of falling prices. Foreign trade was expanding rapidly and foreign investors were thronging at China's doorstep. However, some domestic policy tensions were also becoming apparent. The rate of bank credit expansion was already above nominal income growth and surging higher, there were concerns about bad loans in the banking system, and the PBOC was having to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to maintain the yuan's peg to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 What did that mean for China's monetary policy?\par \par \uc2 China's monetary policy was constrained by the need to keep the yuan's value constant relative to the dollar. The trade surplus and capital inflows were putting pressure on the yuan to appreciate, which could have hurt \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's export competitiveness. The PBOC had started \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to offset these appreciation pressures by buying up dollars and other hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Low interest rates in many advanced economies were preventing the PBOC from raising domestic interest rates as that would draw in even more capital inflows. This also meant that interest rates could not be used to guide banks to tighten or loosen credit conditions; instead, the PBOC had to resort to quantity targets and other means that hampered banking reforms.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Zhou faced challenges with inflation in 2004, 2008 and 2011 - how did he deal with them?\par \par \uc2 In each of these episodes, Mr. Zhou recognized that the expansion of bank credit needed to be controlled and took measures to limit this expansion. Unable to raise interest rates to the level that would be compatible with domestic price stability, he had to resort to measures such as quantity restrictions (setting ceilings for each bank's credit expansion) and higher reserve requirements on banks.\par \par \uc2 In 2005, Mr. Zhou broke the yuan's peg to the dollar - why was that?\par \par \uc2 By 2005, the pressures on monetary policy were ratcheting up. Inflation had begun to come under control after the spike in 2004 but it was clear that the exchange rate regime had become a millstone around the neck of monetary policy. China's interest rates could not deviate too much from U.S. interest rates, which were much lower than the rates that the PBOC would have preferred domestically. There were also increasing pressures on the yuan to appreciate, with the PBOC accumulating over $200 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves each year through its exchange market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . To start on the road to monetary independence, Mr. Zhou convinced China's leaders that it was time to depeg the yuan from the dollar and allow it to appreciate. While there was considerable pressure from the U.S. and other advanced economies for this to happen, Mr. Zhou made the strong argument that such a move would be in China's own interest and that the rate of appreciation could be managed in a manner that would give Chinese exporters time to adjust.\par \par \uc2 What's the relation between China's exchange rate regime and the U.S. financial crisis?\par \par \uc2 China's tightly managed exchange rate regime is one side of global current account imbalances. China's purchases of U.S. Treasuries as part of its exchange market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped keep interest rates lower for longer in the U.S., giving financial institutions cheap money with which they eventually wreaked havoc. While these imbalances were not at the root of the crisis, they played a role in perpetuating financial market excesses in the U.S. that eventually resulted in the global financial crisis. The case that some U.S. policymakers have made that these imbalances were the proximate cause of the crisis is, however, not tenable.\par \par \uc2 Now Mr. Zhou has more flexibility on both the yuan and interest rates - what has changed?\par \par \uc2 Mr. Zhou has been effective at promoting reforms despite the enormous political constraints that the PBOC faces. He has gradually liberalized interest rates but retained a minimum spread between lending and deposit rates to mollify state-owned banks. While the yuan's appreciation has also been gradual, he has promoted steps to increase capital outflows by corporations, households, and institutional investors such as pension funds. This has taken the pressure off by balancing inflows with higher outflows. However, exchange rate and financial system reforms have been far slower than ideal. If -- as expected - he stays on, Mr. Zhou will need to push forward with fuller interest rate liberalization, development of corporate bond markets and other parts of the financial system, and greater discipline on the large state-owned banks.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Zhou has a pretty good record on managing inflation, but has a decade of low interest rates caused other problems?\par \par \uc2 Low interest rates have led to domestically imbalanced growth. Cheap capital, along with subsidized land and energy, has led to investment-intensive growth. The rapid expansion of investment has kept growth strong, even during the period of the financial crisis, but has not been good at generating employment and has had adverse environmental consequences. Low deposit rates have also meant that households are not well compensated for their high saving rates and this has helped to keep private consumption relatively weak. The ratio of private consumption to GDP in China is now far lower than in virtually all other major economies, either advanced or emerging. In the last two years, there are some signs that the imbalances have started to reverse, although at a glacial pace.\par \par \uc2 What are the chances those problems could blow up in the next year or two?\par \par \uc2 China's approach to growth has been simple. Push growth at any cost and deal later with the problems caused in that process. This is a remarkably difficult act that few other economies have managed to maintain, with the problems usually overwhelming growth and often ending in crises. China's economy has many festering problems--including bad loans in the banking system, high levels of provincial government debt and a creaky financial system that is not doing a good job of allocating savings productively. Still, China's policymakers have created enough room in terms of both fiscal and monetary policies that they can cope with short-term adverse developments. The big challenge is to make growth more economically and environmentally sustainable and also more equitable; otherwise, economic and social tensions could become difficult to manage.\par \par \uc2 If Mr. Zhou stays on at the helm of the PBOC, that is a positive sign that China's leadership recognizes the need for continued reforms, especially in terms of the financial system, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime and capital account.\par \par \uc2 -- Tom Orlik\par \par \uc2 Like China Real Time on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and follow us \uc2 Twitter\uc2  for the latest updates. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  -For continuously updated news from The Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at   \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com  }}}\uc2 \f2 . \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 March 13, 2013 05:33 ET (09:33 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130313e93d00312\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
