\par\par \b \uc2 China Should Expand Yuan Trading Band - Ex-PBOC Adviser\b0\par\par\uc2 314 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2013\par 10:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BEIJING--China should expand the daily trading range of its yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to 5% or 7% this year as it moves to achieve its long-stated goal of making the exchange rate regime more flexible, a former policy adviser to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Thursday.\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  currently sets a daily reference or parity rate against the dollar, and it allows the yuan to move 1% above or below that level. It expanded the daily trading range in April last year from 0.5% as part of its long-stated goal of adding flexibility to the exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 Yu Yongding, an economist who was speaking on the sidelines of the annual session of parliament and its advisory body, said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should limit its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Yu, who is influential in Chinese academic circles, doesn't have a policy-making role and his views on the trading band are considerably bolder than those of many central bankers.\par \par \uc2 The yuan stood at 6.2198 to the dollar in late afternoon on Thursday against its parity rate of 6.2785.\par \par \uc2 Policymakers appear to believe the yuan is close to equilibrium, or "fair value," though they say they want to encourage two-way movement in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But there are concerns over possible upward pressure on the exchange rate from capital inflows as major developed countries maintain so-called "super loose" monetary policies, which push down interest rates, to help boost their economy. China's domestic interest rates are relatively higher, and that could attract more inflows of capital from abroad.\par \par \uc2 Though advocating limited market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Mr. Yu said that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  should step up its monitoring of capital inflows amid uncertainties in capital movements this year.\par \par \uc2 Write to Grace Zhu at grace.zhu@dowjones.com [ 07-03-13 0920GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130307e937000fb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
