\par\par \b \uc2 DJ  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Wars Are Back. Best Be Prepared Says RBS\b0\par\par\uc2 By Clare Connaghan and William Kemble-Diaz  \par \uc2 498 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Januar 2013\par 19:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Talk is building of an all-out \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war as policy makers line up to complain that some countries are gaining an unfair trade advantage by depressing their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to improve their competitiveness.\par \par \uc2 But which countries are most likely to get involved if a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war breaks out?\par \par \uc2 In a research note Wednesday, \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2  says it has some of the answers based on a country's willingness to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and its capacity to do so, given the structure of its economy.\par \par \uc2 Among emerging markets, the U.K. bank singles out Malaysia, Thailand, and Chile as three candidates likely to lean against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation, along with the Czech Republic, a relative "newcomer to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation."\par \par \uc2 RBS strategists David Petitcolin and David Simmonds say Sweden, which has traditionally not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets, could also join the melee.\par \par \uc2 In contrast, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's willingness to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  is tempered by the resilience of its exports, while in Brazil exports account for only a small proportion of the overall economy, they say.\par \par \uc2 That sits oddly with Brazil's relatively aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year. Brazil's finance minister Guido Mantega first coined the phrase "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  wars" in 2010 to describe the global fallout from the monetary policy easing in developed countries.\par \par \uc2 He accused the U.S. Federal Reserve and other major central banks of flooding financial markets with cash to depress their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as investors plowed this cash into emerging markets in search of higher returns.\par \par \uc2 Brazil and other emerging markets subsequently took steps to stem these pressures.\par \par \uc2 And more than two years on, the urge to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in check appears to be returning.\par \par \uc2 A new government in Japan looks determined to turn the tide on two decades of deflation and the yen has weakened to levels not seen since June 2010. At the same time, the Federal Reserve continues to pump money into the debt-laden U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 "For the quantitative easing-leading Japan and U.S., \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  competitiveness is not the lead policy obsession," said RBS. "Rather, it's a welcome consequence of the need to loosen financial conditions to reflate domestically."\par \par \uc2 But what's welcome for some countries is not so welcome for others.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's first deputy chairman Alexei Ulyukayev warned that these policies were leading to global imbalances. Meanwhile, Eurogroup head Jean-Claude Juncker spoke late Tuesday of a "dangerously high" euro exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 Even the Bank Of England, which has a history of not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has railed in recent months against the strength of the pound.\par \par \uc2 As RBS puts it: "This year could see [the] \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fracas morph into something more internationally combustible: a global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war."\par \par \uc2 Write to Clare Connaghan at clare.connaghan@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 Subscribe to WSJ: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires }}}\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 16, 2013 13:22 ET (18:22 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130116e91g004o0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
