\par\par \b \uc2 Yuan expected to strengthen mildly in 2013 - People's Daily\b0\par\par\uc2 659 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Januar 2013\par 05:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Report reaffirms Beijing's commitment to a stable rate\par \par \uc2 * Traders and economists expect more volatility in 2013\par \par \uc2 * Macro data does not justify undue optimism - strategist\par \par \uc2 * One-year NDFs forecast depreciation\par \par \uc2 By Pete Sweeney\par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, Jan 4 (Reuters) - The Chinese yuan is expected to log another year of mild strengthening in 2013, the official People's Daily newspaper reported, as market forces cooperate with policymakers to hold back aggressive appreciation.\par \par \uc2 The report, which quoted economists from domestic and foreign banks, is in line with previous official declarations that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has reached a market-driven equilibrium. It also accords with repeated commitments by Beijing to preserve the "fundamental stability" of the exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 In 2012, the yuan appreciated 1 percent against the dollar, less than analysts had forecast at the start of last year.\par \par \uc2 Many mainstream economists and \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders believe that the days of one-way yuan appreciation have already drawn to a close, pointing out that in the current economic and policy environment, the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has become a much more volatile instrument.\par \par \uc2 China's current-account surplus as a share of gross domestic product, which foreign governments pointed to in the past as evidence that Beijing was suppressing the yuan's exchange rate, has narrowed significantly in recent years.\par \par \uc2 The surplus has dropped from more than 10 percent of GDP in 2007 to below 3 percent in the first three quarters of 2012.\par \par \uc2 As a result, the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  now describes the yuan as only "moderately undervalued."\par \par \uc2 At the same time, while China's economy continues to recover, few expect it to post an explosive turnaround in 2013.\par \par \uc2 Dariusz Kowalczyk, strategist at \uc2 Credit Agricole\uc2  CIB in Hong Kong, predicted appreciation of more than 2 percent for the yuan in 2013, which would put it at 6.1 per dollar by the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 "Stronger gains are unlikely given the shallowness of economic recovery," he wrote in a research note distributed on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 A VOLATILE 2012\par \par \uc2 In the first seven months of 2012, the spot yuan actually lost 1.6 percent against the dollar in the face of the euro debt crisis.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  then recovered dramatically in the latter portion of the year as optimism about a soft-landing in China caused corporates to stock back up on yuan. The increased appetite combined with other factors to flood the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market with unwanted dollars, pushing the yuan to consecutive record highs against the dollar in November.\par \par \uc2 At that point, the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  (PBOC) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to preserve stability, holding back the yuan through conservative midpoint settings.\par \par \uc2 Since last April, the exchange rate has been allowed to diverge by 1 percent above or below the official midpoint rate , double the previous range.\par \par \uc2 Chia Woon Khien, strategist for emerging Asia at the \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2 , said that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely widen the band again in 2013.\par \par \uc2 "Now it's 2 percent, plus or minus one, but that's not the last stop to a free float. I think there will be another half percent and another half percent."\par \par \uc2 Such reforms, especially if combined with further liberalisations to China's capital account facilitating cross-border flows, would likely result in even more volatility in the exchange rate than seen in 2012.\par \par \uc2 Depreciation may also be in the cards. Traders say the yuan will likely move in a range between 6.1 and 6.3 in 2013, implying periods of appreciation and depreciation.\par \par \uc2 Offshore one-year non-deliverable forwards, which serve as an imperfect proxy for market expectations for yuan appreciation or depreciation, continue to forecast mild depreciation over the next 12 months.\par \par \uc2 The yuan has appreciated nearly 25 percent against the dollar since 2005.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Adam Jourdan; Editing by Richard Borsuk)\par \par \uc2 CHINA-YUAN-APPRECIATION/ | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | ABX | BNX | FUN | Z | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130104e9140004n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
