\par\par \b \uc2 China money rates bounce, liquidity remains loose\b0\par\par\uc2 470 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Dezember 2012\par 05:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * China money rates rebound after week fall\par \par \uc2 * 7-day rate up over 30 bps from 2-week low\par \par \uc2 * But traders say market liquidity loose\par \par \uc2 By Adam Jourdan\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, Dec 14 (Reuters) - China's key seven-day money rate rebounded on Friday morning from a two-week intra-day low on Thursday, though traders said that borrowing conditions remained loose.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark weighted-average seven-day bond repurchase rate rose to 3.0040 percent from 2.6789 percent at the close on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Money rates have been pulled in opposite directions this week, as a year-end cash squeeze puts upward pressure on demand, while suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market pumped more yuan into the system, easing supply.\par \par \uc2 "It's quite strange really that the rates have gone up today as borrowing conditions in the market still seem pretty loose," said a trader at a commercial bank in Shanghai.\par \par \uc2 "Over the year-end, people are very willing to lend so demand pressure is not all that great."\par \par \uc2 The 14-day repo rate edged up to 3.0182 percent from 2.9734 percent, while the one-day rate\par \par \uc2 fell slightly to 2.2653 percent from 2.2843 percent.\par \par \uc2 The 21-day rate shot up 67.18 basis points to 4.3684, its highest level since late October, though traders say that low volume of trading at the tenor means the rate can be moved by a small number of transactions.\par \par \uc2 Rates had fallen widely earlier in the week as \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders suspected that the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to rein in yuan spot rate\par \par \uc2 appreciation, leading to a rash of dollar-buying by major state-owned banks.\par \par \uc2 Such dollar purchases boost yuan liquidity, as the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  uses newly-created yuan to buy dollars.\par \par \uc2 Though there is no hard evidence that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is directing the market, traders say the pattern of major state-owned banks aggressively bidding for dollars - as seen earlier this week - is a likely sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  involvement.\par \par \uc2 They say the bank had become uncomfortable with strong yuan appreciation, which would likely have extended into December had the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  not forcibly restrained further growth by holding back the official midpoint.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  put the brakes on rate falls through a weekly net 125 billion yuan drain from the market through open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the largest net drain in nearly two months.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                               Current  Prev close  Change\par                                     (pct)           (bps)  \par 7-day repo         3.0040      2.6789    + 32.51\par 7-day SHIBOR           3.0000      2.6600    + 34.00  \par Note: Repo rate is weighted average.\par   \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ($1 = 6.2329 Chinese yuan)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Editing by Sanjeev Miglani)\f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-CHINA-BONDS/ | ABX | ABN | D | M | RBN | Z | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020121214e8ce0005u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
