\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 4-U.S. declines to name China  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  manipulator\b0\par\par\uc2 929 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 November 2012\par 22:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds Chinese government comment)\par \par \uc2 * Yuan up 12.6 pct in real terms vs dollar since June 2010\par \par \uc2 * U.S. still sees significant undervaluation\par \par \uc2 * China last labeled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator 18 years ago\par \par \uc2 * China denies \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  undervalued\par \par \uc2 By Anna Yukhananov\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON, Nov 27 (Reuters) - The Obama administration said on Tuesday that China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained "significantly undervalued," but stopped short of labeling the world's second-biggest economy a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator.\par \par \uc2 Although Beijing controls the pace at which the yuan can rise, the \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  said in a congressionally mandated semi-annual report that China did not meet the legal requirements to be deemed a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator.\par \par \uc2 The label is largely symbolic, but would require Washington to open discussions with Beijing on adjusting the yuan's value.\par \par \uc2 It has been 18 years since the \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  has designated any country a manipulator. China was labeled a manipulator between 1992 and 1994.\par \par \uc2 The latest report reflected both the administration's desire to maintain good relations with its top creditor and an attempt to keep up pressure for changes in China that could benefit the U.S. economy and mollify domestic critics.\par \par \uc2 The report noted that the yuan, also known as the renminbi, had risen 12.6 percent against the U.S. dollar in inflation-adjusted terms since June 2010. An official said it was up 9.7 percent on a nominal basis through Tuesday, when it closed at a record high.\par \par \uc2 The Treasury also said \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  had "substantially" reduced its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since the third quarter of 2011 and had loosened capital controls.\par \par \uc2 "In light of these developments, Treasury has concluded that the standards ... have not been met with respect to China," it said.\par \par \uc2 "Nonetheless, the available evidence suggests the renminbi remains significantly undervalued," the report added, echoing the Treasury's last assessment in May.\par \par \uc2 Chinese Foreign Ministry spokesman Hong Lei denied the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was undervalued.\par \par \uc2 "In recent years, the ratio between China's GDP and the current account surplus has decreased on a daily basis. The renminbi's exchange rate is in equilibrium. There is no so-called problem that the exchange rate is undervalued," he told reporters in Beijing.\par \par \uc2 "We hope that the U.S. side can appropriately deal with trade and economic issues, including the renminbi exchange rate," Hong added.\par \par \uc2 Ted Truman, a Treasury official under former President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 , said it was important to keep a watchful eye on China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.\par \par \uc2 "We have the aftermath of 10 years of misbehavior," said Truman, who is now with the Peterson Institute for International Economics. "It would probably be unwise and too soon to declare victory."\par \par \uc2 During the U.S. presidential campaign, Republican candidate \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  pledged to label China a manipulator on his first day in office to show he would be tougher on the chief U.S. economic competitor than President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Many U.S. businesses and lawmakers complain that Beijing keeps the value of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  artificially low to gain an advantage in trade at the expense of American jobs.\par \par \uc2 But an international consensus is growing that the yuan is closing in on its fair value after about a decade at an artificially weak level. The \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  softened its language on the yuan in July.\par \par \uc2 YUAN AT RECORD HIGH\par \par \uc2 Signs of a recovery in the Chinese economy and a new round of quantitative easing by the U.S. Federal Reserve have led traders to push the yuan higher.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has kept a lid on the move. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  allows the yuan to rise or fall by only 1 percent from whatever rate it sets each day.\par \par \uc2 Charles Schumer, the No. 3 Democrat in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  and a longtime critic of China's yuan policy, said the Treasury passed up an opportunity to level the trade playing field.\par \par \uc2 "It's time for the Obama administration to rip off the band-aid, and force China to play by the same rules as all other countries," the New York senator said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 But the \uc2 U.S.-China Business Council\uc2 , which represents U.S. companies that do business with China, applauded the decision.\par \par \uc2 "The exchange rate has little to do with the U.S. trade balance or employment," council President John Frisbie said. "We need to move on to more important issues with China, such as removing market access barriers and improving intellectual property protection."\par \par \uc2 The Treasury said further appreciation of the yuan would help China balance its economy toward consumption by giving households greater purchasing power.\par \par \uc2 It called on China to reduce its "exceptionally high" \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and publish data about its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 The Obama administration also used the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  report to keep pressure on South Korea to limit its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 South Korea says it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth the volatility of its won \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but it has gone into the market throughout 2012, the Treasury report said. In July, the IMF said the won was undervalued by up to 10 percent.\par \par \uc2 "We will continue to press the Korean authorities to limit their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the exceptional circumstances of disorderly market conditions," the report said.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Ben Blanchard in BEIJING; Editing by James Dalgleish & Kim Coghill)\par \par \uc2 USA-CHINA/TREASURY (UPDATE 4) | C | D | E | M | O | U | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL | G | RBN | J | ABX | BNX | FUN | RNA | AFA | CSA | LBY | RWSA | RWS | REULB | GNS | Z | RNP | DNP | PGE   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020121127e8br0016i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
