\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: China a Wild Card if BoJ Opts to Devalue Yen\b0\par\par\uc2 1,456 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 November 2012\par 15:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 9:48 EST - The world's biggest \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -trading bank flags an interesting note--the BoJ's road to weaken the yen might give China a reason to reject G7 pressure for a flexible yuan exchange-rate regime as a falling yen makes Japan's exports more competitive in global marketplace. \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  notes the US would be particularly concerned if Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was used by \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  to justify a more-\b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  approach given the obvious sensitivities of the large US-\b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  bilateral imbalance. Further complicating the matter is if a new Japan government takes a more-nationalistic approach to relations with China, Japan would be in need of US political support. But "open attempts to pursue a weaker yen would come with an unacceptably steep political price for Japan." (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:48 EST - Brazil's real, which looked set to weaken past 2.1 per USD, strengthened a bit after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it's changing the way it carries out \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. Though the changes are minimal, traders interpreted it as a message that "you think we're not watching, but we see what's going on" in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, says \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader Reginaldo Galhardo at Treviso brokerage. Some nervous traders -- who in recent days tested the bank's willingness to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- likely also reversed bets after the statement, thinking the bank was preparing to step into markets, Galhardo says. (paulo.winterstein@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:44 EST - Remember, the auction comes earlier than usual, at 11:30 am EST to accommodate market participants jetting off for Thanksgiving. Nomura's rate strategists expect the $13B inflation-protected 10-year note offering to "go well" as its premium to nominal 10-year notes has come down since mid-September, when the Fed announced unlimited MBS QE. The gap between those two yields is 2.40 percentage points recently, from 2.68 then. Those concerned about the fiscal cliff--which would lead to more aggressive Fed easing--should also be interested in buying TIPS today. (cynthia.lin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:37 EST - With inventory levels at the big 3 teen-apparel retailers--Aeropostale (ARO), American Eagle (AEO) and Abercrombie (ANF)--at a their lowest entering-the-holidays level in 3 years, ARO could benefit most, says Cowen. The investment bank notes the company's traffic has been hit by the "heavy clearance inventory" at ANF the past year. With inventories more in check sectorwide, "ARO's SSS and gross margin could surpass Street expectations given an improving fashion mix and easy comparisons into 2013." As such, multiple expansion could soon be in order, says Cowen. (anna.prior@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:37 EST - Campbell's (CPB) FY1Q results show the soup maker "has not yet turned the corner," and that "business momentum is lacking," RBC says in assessment of yesterday's report. While soup sales were a bit better than expected, other businesses like foodservice and beverages underperformed. The firm does give credit to CPB for better managing prices in its soup business, and the early returns from the Bolthouse acquisition. But as of now, it doesn't see anything in results that suggest that CPB's "valuation discount" to peers should narrow. Shares off a fraction at $36.15. (paul.ziobro@dowjones.com; @pziobro)\par \par \uc2 9:28 EST - Deere's (DE) F4Q margins disappoint as revenue was stronger than expected but EPS missed, with the letdown mainly on the agricultural side of the business, William Blair says. "The sentiment on Deere has increased recently with the improving U.S. agriculture outlook, and thus expectations were for a moderate beat," firm writes, noting "agriculture and turf operating margin was 12.6%, compared to consensus of 13.5%." William Blair also says weak guidance, with 2013 net income forecast below Street views, will weigh on shares. "With expectations high going into the report, the guidance can only be viewed as negative. However, we also believe that the guidance will ultimately end up being conservative." DE down 3.3% premarket. (john.shipman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:26 EST - Jobless claims remain elevated and Greece still doesn't have its hands on more aid, but safe-haven Treasury are slipping as investors unwind some of the market's overbought conditions ahead of Thanksgiving. Meanwhile, Andrew Brenner of National Alliance Capital Markets has interpreted a hint of optimism in Bernanke's speech Tuesday. "He's a little more optimism about the economy," says Brenner, who doesn't see the Fed committing to more Treasurys purchases for next year. Bernanke seems to "expect a really good year if the fiscal cliff gets resolved, and a good year doesn't mean for good for guys long Treasurys." Benchmark 10-year notes are down 9/32, yielding 1.688%. (cynthia.lin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:23 EST - Sandy's aftermath and concerns over the "fiscal cliff" have \uc2 Barclays\uc2  lowering its holiday season sales growth estimate for broadline/department stores to 1.5%-2.5% from 2.5%-3%, and compared with a 2.9% increase last year. Several stores were closed earlier this month due to the "superstorm," but the impact will be largely offset by two extra selling days and big Black Friday numbers, firm says. \uc2 Barclays\uc2  also sees a 3%-3.5% decline at department stores, driven entirely by an estimated 25% comp drop at JCPenney (JCP) due to continued "challenging" trends from company's pricing strategy. "This decline accounts for much of the revision in our overall holiday comp estimate" as well, firm says. (karen.talley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:22 EST - The yen has been hammered lately on prospects of more-aggressive BoJ actions to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and rescue the economy. \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  agrees the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy actions will be key driver--but it cautions the yen will likely fluctuate throughout 2013 amid expectations and disappointments around the steps taken and not continue a straight-line drop. Its base case is that the dollar is unlikely to break out of the Y75-85 range that has been in place for more than 2 year. But \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  sees 15% odds the dollar jumps to Y90 if Japan's inflation expectations heighten significantly, and a similar 15% chance of it falling to Y75 if the US falls from its own fiscal cliff and concerns about European peripheral countries heighten significantly. The dollar is up 1% today at Y82.46. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:17 EST - The Teranet-National Bank Composite House Price Index fell 0.25% in October, the 11th-consecutive monthly decline, though it is less than September's drop. Seven of the 11 urban areas the index tracks saw falling prices last month, with Toronto--Canada's largest city--logging the most -pronounced drop after Quebec City. That might be a sign the federal government's adjustment of Canadian mortgage regulations several months ago has done its part in cooling off some of Canada's hottest housing markets. (david.george-cosh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:13 EST - \uc2 Miller Tabak\uc2 's Andrew Wilkinson estimates Hurricane Sandy indirectly accounted for 83,895 initial jobless claims in the latest week. Working back from the prior week's reading of 451,000, that would put this week's ex-Sandy number around 367,000 initial claims, vs the 410,000 the Labor Dept reported, Wilkinson says. While some of those claims might have still occurred absent Sandy, it shows the job market is still growing at a modest pace seen in recent months and falls in line with where claims were earlier this fall. As Fed chair Bernanke pointed out in a speech yesterday, unemployment remains elevated and needs to come down for the Fed to tighten policy. (stephen.bernard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:02 EST - Yet another "fat finger" problem on Wall Street? Nasdaq MarketWatch is investigating "potentially erroneous transactions," this time involving shares of \uc2 American Reprographics Company\uc2  (ARC), a provider of document services to companies. Between 7:26am and 7:52am ET, the stock traded as high as $23.02 in the premarket, or more than nine times yesterday's closing price of $2.50. The stock closed at an all-time low of $2.49 on Monday, and hasn't traded as high as $23 since Oct. 25, 2007. (tomi.kilgore@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Bradley Davis, 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 11-21-12 0948ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020121121e8bl0005q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
