\par\par \b \uc2 Yuan hits record high as c.bank loosens reins slightly\b0\par\par\uc2 558 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 November 2012\par 10:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Yuan hits record highs versus dollar\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 PBOC\uc2  sets strongest fix in six months\par \par \uc2 * Yuan scrapes along strong-side limit to close\par \par \uc2 * Positive domestic data supports yuan rise\par \par \uc2 (Updates to close)\par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, Nov 12 (Reuters) - Buoyed by positive economic data, China's yuan rose to a record high on Monday as persistent dollar selling by Chinese corporates drove the spot rate to its maximum daily limit after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set an unusually strong midpoint rate.\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \uc2 fixed the midpoint\par \par \uc2 at 6.2920 per dollar - the strongest fix since May - and sharply up on Friday's midpoint of 6.3012.\par \par \uc2 But the market was even more bullish than the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as it has been for nearly a month now, and the spot yuan rate\par \par \uc2 swiftly hit the 1 percent limit of its trading band to repeatedly trade at or around 6.2291 against the dollar until the close, for a gain of 0.25 percent on the day.\par \par \uc2 Under China's managed float, the dollar/yuan rate is allowed to diverge 1 percent in either direction from the official midpoint each day.\par \par \uc2 Monday's market open, traded levels and the record close marked the strongest trading day since China opened its domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in 1994.\par \par \uc2 Traders said that positive domestic data, including stronger-than-expected export growth and recent purchasing manager index (PMI) figures, had boosted already strong confidence in the yuan.\par \par \uc2 "Improving PMI and trade data helped convince the market that the yuan still still has room to appreciate," said a trader at an international bank in Shanghai.\par \par \uc2 The yuan is up more than 2.6 percent from a weak point it struck in late July, and stands more than 1 percent up for the year-to-date.\par \par \uc2 But traders say the yuan would be trading even stronger if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not keeping the market in check through its midpoint fixes, which have routinely proven more conservative than the market feels comfortable with.\par \par \uc2 Some market analysts believe the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should bow to market pressure by widening the trading band, while others say the dollar selling will subside soon and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can afford to wait until \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's incoming leaders roll out more reforms.\par \par \uc2 SUSPECTED \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Traders said they suspect that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  through major state banks to buy up dollars at the end of last week.\par \par \uc2 "There was a wave of dollar buying from major state-owned banks on Friday, but some traders suspected it was actually \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  to absorb dollars," said a trader at a European bank in Shanghai.\par \par \uc2 Market participants offer different explanations for the yuan's strength, which has continued despite the dollar's gains against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , reflected in the rise of the dollar index since mid-October.\par \par \uc2 Some market analysts believe Chinese corporate treasurers may be rebalancing their positions after going too long in dollars in the first half of the year.\par \par \uc2 Others say the dollar selling partly reflects an unexpectedly strong recovery in export growth, which hit a five-month high in October.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Shanghai Newsroom; Editing by Pete Sweeney & Kim Coghill)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-CHINA-YUAN-CLOSE/ | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | ABX | BNX | FUN | Z | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020121115e8bc000il\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
