\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECTED-Yuan firms on state bank buying; c.bank  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  suspected\b0\par\par\uc2 392 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Oktober 2012\par 06:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  (Corrects yuan rate in bullet to 6.2486/dlr)\par \par \uc2  * Yuan gains 0.1 pct to 6.2486/dlr near midday\par \par \uc2  * BOC seen buying yuan aggressively\par \par \uc2  * C.bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspected, not confirmed\par \par \uc2  * Corporate dollar demand weak amid yuan appreciation trend \par \par \uc2  By Gabriel Wildau\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Oct 23 (Reuters) - China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  notched higher on Tuesday morning as traders reported aggressive yuan buying by a major state bank and weak corporate demand for dollars.\par \par \uc2  The yuan set its daily midpoint virtually flat at 6.3078 versus the dollar, little changed from Monday's fix of 6.3077. But spot yuan traded at 6.2486 near midday, a gain of 0.1 percent from Monday's close.\par \par \uc2  A trader at a city commercial bank in Shanghai said \uc2 Bank of China\uc2  , one of China's "big four" state-owned commercial banks, was selling dollars aggressively in morning trade, pushing the yuan higher.\par \par \uc2  Traders have frequently cited BOC in recent weeks as a major source of yuan demand. Such reports bolster the view of analysts who believe that the yuan's recent strength is due in part to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  (PBOC)\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  The \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets typically occur via trades with major state banks such as BOC. But such trades are usually not visible to the rest of the market, so traders can only guess whether the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  is behind the market moves.\par \par \uc2  The yuan demand from BOC came on top of weak demand for dollars from corporate clients over the last two days, traders said.\par \par \uc2  The yuan has rallied 2.4 percent since hitting a year low of 6.3967 on July 25. The rally has caused many Chinese importers and exporters to reduce long dollar positions built up earlier in the year, when the yuan was depreciating. \par \par \uc2  With the appreciation trend now in place for nearly three months, exporters are inclined to trade most of their dollar receipts for yuan, traders and analysts say.\par \par \uc2  The midpoint is the daily reference rate from which \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  allows the dollar/yuan rate to rise or fall by 1 percent in a single day.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Eric Meijer)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-CHINA-YUAN-MIDDAY/ (CORRECTED) | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | ABX | BNX | FUN | Z | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020121026e8an00058\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
