\par\par \b \uc2 Yuan ends flat in narrow trade, traders eye euro for clues\b0\par\par\uc2 467 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Oktober 2012\par 11:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Yuan trades in narrow range, reflecting uncertainty\par \par \uc2 * Yuan could climb higher if euro's recovery continues\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 PBOC\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  not main cause of yuan's recent strength\par \par \uc2 * Yuan recovery forced corporates out of long USD positions\par \par \uc2 (Updates to close)\par \par \uc2 By Gabriel Wildau\par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, Oct 22 (Reuters) - China's yuan closed flat against the dollar on Monday, with trading confined to a narrow range as the market eyes the euro for clues on whether the yuan will scale new heights this week.\par \par \uc2 After touching a series of record highs in the last two weeks, traders say the yuan's near-term trajectory now depends on the course of the euro. If the euro and other non-dollar \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  continue to strengthen against the dollar, the yuan could follow it upward to new historical highs.\par \par \uc2 Traders also believe the U.S. election could persuade the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  to set somewhat stronger fixings in the coming weeks, but the effect is unlikely to override the impact of client demand and the trend in the dollar globally, as measured by the dollar index.\par \par \uc2 Spot yuan closed at 6.2547, little changed from Friday's close of 6.2538.\par \par \uc2 Before trade began, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set its daily midpoint\par \par \uc2 slightly weaker at 6.3071 per dollar, the second straight day of mildly weaker fixings following six straight days of stronger midpoints through last Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Traders largely dismissed speculation about the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  (PBOC) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yuan amid heated rhetoric in the U.S. presidential campaign about China's alleged \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation.\par \par \uc2 Instead, most believe corporate demand and the recovery of the euro following its mid-summer lows are responsible for the yuan's recent strength. The yuan touched its most recent all-time intraday high at 6.2446 last Thursday.\par \par \uc2 "In the previous month, the yuan had already appreciated by about 1,000 pips. At that point, there's no reason for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to step in and cause even more appreciation," said a trader at Chinese joint stock bank in Shanghai, referring to the yuan's movement over the last two weeks.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in the year, when the yuan was depreciating, many corporates accumulated large long dollar positions. But when the yuan began to rebound, these clients faced pressure to sell dollars in order to avoid excessive losses on those positions.\par \par \uc2 "Before the yuan fell by too much, so there was some correction. Then after the yuan had already recovered by, say, 500 pips, these guys couldn't hold on anymore. So of course a lot of client orders appeared," the trader said.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Jacqueline Wong)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-CHINA-YUAN-CLOSE/ | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | ABX | BNX | FUN | Z | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020121025e8am0007y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
