\par\par \b \uc2 Is U.S. criticism or China's economy behind yuan rally?\b0\par\par\uc2 1,138 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Oktober 2012\par 12:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  * Yuan rallies to record high against dollar\par \par \uc2  * Rally began in July, picks up speed in last week\par \par \uc2  * Acceleration sparks speculation of political motives\par \par \uc2  * U.S. presidential candidates have argued over yuan value\par \par \uc2  * Higher yuan could help China blunt criticism \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  undervalued \par \par \uc2  By Gabriel Wildau\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Oct 19 (Reuters) - China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a record high this week in a rally that has been accelerating, prompting speculation over whether Beijing is behind the move to try to dampen criticism in the U.S. presidential debate that the yuan is undervalued.\par \par \uc2  Washington has been a persistent thorn in China's side, complaining for years that the government keeps the yuan's exchange rate artificially low to boost its exports and grab market share, ultimately costing American jobs.\par \par \uc2  "I don't disagree that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy could be behind the yuan's recent rise," said Zhang Zhiming, head of China research for \uc2 HSBC\uc2 . "But on the other hand, it's not like there are no alternative explanations that can support a strong yuan without policy buying."\par \par \uc2  Most dealers say there are plenty of economic factors to justify a rally that began in July, such as a pick up in the country's trade surplus.\par \par \uc2  At most the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have used higher daily fixings in recent weeks to signal its tolerance for a higher exchange rate, which could help blunt criticism repeated in the U.S. presidential debate that the yuan is undervalued.\par \par \uc2  Claudio Piron, head of emerging Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and fixed-income strategy at \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2 , said higher yuan fixings could be a response to the U.S. campaign rhetoric but other elements are probably driving the rally.\par \par \uc2  "It's a nice story, but I think there are other factors that suggest there are limitations to how far it can run," he said.\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  (PBOC) \uc2 has historically controlled the yuan closely through \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and by setting a reference rate for the start of trading each day, known as the daily midpoint.\par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is limited to a daily trading range against the dollar of 1 percent either side of the midpoint.\par \par \uc2  That means the yuan moves more slowly compared with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of more open economies, which is why a 2.4 percent rally from a 2012 low in July to a record high on Thursday of 6.2446 per dollar raised eye brows, e sp ecially as it coincided with deepening concern over the prospects for the world's second-biggest economy.\par \par \uc2  CALCULATING ROMNEY?\par \par \uc2  As the rally entered September, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared keen to restrain the rise, frequently s etting the daily midpoint at a weaker level than the day-earlier close.\par \par \uc2  Last week, it changed course, setting stronger midpoints as if to endorse the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength and so raising market speculation there was a political motive behind the trend.\par \par \uc2  "We continue to believe that the strong fixings may reflect China's desire to support the current U.S. administration ahead of the U.S. elections in November," Dariusz Kowalczyk, senior economist at \uc2 Credit Agricole\uc2  CIB, wrote in a research note on Monday.\par \par \uc2  The argument is that China would prefer President Barack Obama to stay in office because Republican challenger Mitt Romney has promised a tougher line on Beijing on trade and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  Romney reiterated this week in a televised debate with Obama that he would label China a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator on day one of his presidency if elected on November 6.\par \par \uc2  Obama defended his tough-on-China credentials. "As far as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation, the (Chinese) \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has actually gone up 11 percent since I've been president because we have pushed them hard," he said in the debate.\par \par \uc2  Analysts note that the consequences of the "manipulator" label are less severe than commonly understood anyway.\par \par \uc2  "The dirty little secret here is that labelling China a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator just triggers consultations on the issue.  Which is why, actually, I think Romney is pushing this - he knows it doesn't mean much," wrote Simon Lester, trade policy analyst at the Cato Institute, a Washington, D.C.-based think tank that favours libertarian economic policy, including free trade.\par \par \uc2  "Look at the language Romney uses here.  He says he will do this (sanctions) 'if necessary'. Here, then, he leaves himself wiggle room. He can take some time to negotiate with China, study the issue, and ultimately decide that the tariffs are unnecessary," Lester wrote.\par \par \uc2  The \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  said earlier this year that labelling China a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator was not warranted because the yuan had risen against the dollar, China's trade surplus had dropped and Beijing was committed to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reforms.\par \par \uc2  Last week it chose to delay issuing its latest review on the subject until after the election, prompting further criticism from the Romney campaign.\par \par \uc2  STRONG EXPORTS, WEAK DOLLAR\par \par \uc2  Dealers suggest factors closer to home explain the rise in the yuan.\par \par \uc2  Trade surpluses in the four months of June through September picked up sharply compared with the first five months of the year, rising to $111 billion from about $38 billion.\par \par \uc2  That contributed to a rise in China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of $50 billion in the third quarter, reversing much of the previous quarter's $65 billion drain. \par \par \uc2 Barclays\uc2  says that outflows of "hot money", or speculative funds, slowed down in the third quarter $54 billion from $166 billion in the previous quarter.\par \par \uc2  Indeed, economists say there may have been a net inflow of "hot money" in the quarter as the U.S. Federal Reserve's third-round of quantitative easing delivered funds into global markets.\par \par \uc2  Some of that money could be backing bets in China that a new leadership due to be unveiled in November will be more aggressive in trying to lift the economy. Hong Kong's Hang Seng index has rallied for seven straight weeks.\par \par \uc2  In addition, a factor pushing the yuan higher since late July has been the recovery of the euro and other non-dollar \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the wake of the Fed's QE3 and aggressive action by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the euro zone debt crisis.\par \par \uc2  Last week, PBOC Deputy Governor Yi Gang said the yuan is close to its equilibrium level and that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has "dramatically reduced" its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market.\par \par \uc2  Analysts say Yi's comments and the rise in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves suggest the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market even if the rise in the yuan helps China fend off criticism over its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  "I think the political consideration is there too," \uc2 UBS\uc2  economist Wang Tao said. \par \par \uc2 (Editing by Jason Subler and Neil Fullick)\par \par \uc2 CHINA-YUAN/ | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | ABX | BNX | FUN | Z | RNP | DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020121019e8aj000m8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
