\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2  c.bank says "dramatically reduced" yuan  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 773 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Oktober 2012\par 14:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * yuan near equilibrium, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  deputy head says\par \par \uc2 * yuan driven by market, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "dramatically reduced"\par \par \uc2 (Adds details, quotes)\par \par \uc2 By Koh Gui Qing\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 12 (Reuters) - China argued on Friday that the yuan is close to its equilibrium level as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has "dramatically reduced" its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, rebutting criticism that Beijing suppresses the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help exports.\par \par \uc2 China \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's deputy governor Yi Gang said the fact that gyrations in the yuan, also known as the renminbi, were now dictated by market demand and supply implied it was close to equilibrium, a level he said is otherwise tough to determine.\par \par \uc2 In unusually frank remarks, Yi said he expected China's economy to grow in the "neighbourhood" of 7.8 percent this year with annual inflation seen hitting around 2.7 percent, well under the government's 4 p ercent tar get.\par \par \uc2 Addressing investor concerns about the health of the world's No. 2 economy, Yi said China's property market has stabilised and that Beijing was trying to balance competing needs of squashing speculation, and supporting a key pillar of the economy.\par \par \uc2 "I am glad to report to you that the renminbi exchange rate is very close to its equilibrium level," Yi said at a conference on the sidelines of \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  and \uc2 World Bank\uc2  meetings in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 "People might not know that the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has dramatically reduced the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market place. For the past more than one year, the official reserves of China have been flat," he said.\par \par \uc2 China's $3.3 trillion \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves have indeed hovered around the same level in the past year after leaping eight times in the last eight years to become the world's largest stockpile.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say China's huge export revenues and its undervalued \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  contributed to the rapid build-up. Yi heads the \uc2 State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulator which manages the reserves.\par \par \uc2 Still, the yuan remains tightly controlled by Beijing. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixes a mid-point for the yuan every trading day, from which the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  can rise or fall just 1 percent.\par \par \uc2 China's trade partners, especially the United States, have long argued that Beijing deliberately holds down the yuan for unfair trade advantage. China has always denied these accusations.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  has said the yuan was moderately undervalued.\par \par \uc2 The yuan crept to a new closing high against the dollar on Friday for a second day after a stronger fixing of the mid-point.\par \par \uc2 PROPERTY DILEMMA\par \par \uc2 China's property market, which accounts for 13 percent of its gross domestic product and affects over 40 other sectors, is mired in its worst slowdown in three years.\par \par \uc2 A slowing Chinese economy and measures taken by the government to stamp out speculation have hit the housing market, to the growing alarm of some economists who fear Beijing is over-asserting itself and hurting legitimate sources of growth.\par \par \uc2 Yi acknowledged those concerns on Friday in a rare admission\par \par \uc2 of Beijing's property dilemma.\par \par \uc2 "Housing prices in Beijing and Shanghai, the large cities in China, are already very high. I think, you see, we should consider policy measures to prevent a housing bubble. That is definitely not the scenario we want to see," Yi said.\par \par \uc2 "On the other hand, we also realise that housing (is) a very important industry in terms of growth and also related to consumption," he said. "So we are in a very delicate situation to maintain the stability."\par \par \uc2 China's house prices were scaling record highs before the government, worried that unaffordable housing would trigger social unrest, acted forcefully to stamp out exuberance.\par \par \uc2 Down payments were raised, property taxes introduced, and the number of homes Chinese can buy were limited.\par \par \uc2 Yi said Beijing would continue to do what it can by building more low-cost public homes, but added: "We have to respect market forces".\par \par \uc2 Compared to a year ago, China's house prices are still falling but a gentle recovery has taken hold on a month-to-month basis. Prices rose 0.1 percent in August from July.\par \par \uc2 China's economic growth is limping at three-year lows and analysts polled by Reuters in September estimated full-year growth will stand at 7.7 percent, the weakest in 13 years but still above Beijing's 7.5 percent target.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Koh Gui Qing; Writing by Emily Kaiser; Editing Neil Fullick and Tomasz Janowski)\par \par \uc2 IMF-CHINA/  (UPDATE 1) | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | ABX | BNX | FUN | Z | RNP | DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020121012e8ac000v5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
