\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-China sees basic balance in external payments in H2\b0\par\par\uc2 414 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 September 2012\par 03:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * China H1 current account surplus 2.1 pct of GDP\par \par \uc2 * SAFE sees more fluctuations in capital flows\par \par \uc2 (Adds details, comments)\par \par \uc2 BEIJING, Sept 12 (Reuters) - China will achieve basic balance in its external payments in the second half of 2012, with fluctuations in two-way capital flows becoming more frequent as the yuan nears its equilibrium level, the top \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulator said on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 China swung into a capital and financial account deficit in the second quarter of $41.2 billion, after a surplus of $56.1 billion in the first quarter and a deficit of $29.0 billion in the fourth quarter of 2011, data from the \uc2 State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  (SAFE) showed.\par \par \uc2 The capital and financial account surplus stood at $14.9 billion in the first six months of 2012, SAFE said.\par \par \uc2 "When the international payment and yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate near reasonable and balanced levels, fluctuations in two-way cross-border capital flows become inevitable," SAFE said in a report on its website, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.safe.gov.cn"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.safe.gov.cn}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "With the improvement in yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  formation mechanism and reduced \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it will become normal for \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  to have current account surplus and capital and financial account deficit," it added.\par \par \uc2 SAFE revised down the current account surplus in the second quarter to $53.7 billion from an initial $59.7 billion.\par \par \uc2 In the first half of 2012, China's current account surplus totalled $77.2 billion, equivalent to 2.1 percent of the country's gross domestic product, down from 2.8 percent in the first six months of 2011.\par \par \uc2 "The falling surplus in international payments and slower rise in China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are in line with the direction of the country's macro control policy and is conducive to promoting external economic balance," SAFE said.\par \par \uc2 It played down the risks of rising capital swings to the economy, citing the country's large \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and steady trade surplus, as well the fact the economy was growing.\par \par \uc2 The report concluded that as long as there were no major risk events at home or abroad, "our country will achieve basic balance in international payments."\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Kevin Yao and Langi Chiang; Editing by John Mair)\par \par \uc2 CHINA-ECONOMY/BOP (UPDATE 1) | ABN | C | GRO | MTL | SOF | D | E | RBN | G | M | O | OIL | Z | ABX | BNX | FUN | RNP | DNP | PTD | PGE | PCM | PEN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120912e89c0002m\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 (foreign exchange or fx or forex or currenc*) and (interven* or operation*) and (china near10 interven*) and (rst=trtw or rst=tprw or rst=tdjw)and (central bank or ministry of finance or treasury ministry or monetary authority)\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\a